2901:
** κρᾰταιό-ω**,
**strengthen**, τὰς χεῖράς τινος Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.1Ki.23.16, +others:—_passive_, {{NT|[prev. work] 30.6, +others, NT.Luke.1.80, “NT.1Cor.16.13”}}, etc.; κ. ὑπέρ τινα **to be too strong for** him, **prevail against** him, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11, +others}}
**2** _passive_, **to be determined**, κ. τοῦ πορεύεσθαι {{LXX|LXX.Ruth.1.18.}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _active_, **prevail**, ἐπί τινας {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.11.23}}; ὑπέρ τινας {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.21(20).23.}}
**III** _middle_, **control**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|πάθη λόγῳ 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.420}}.

2902:
** κρᾰτ-έω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **κρετέω**, _aorist_ _infinitive_ **κρέτησαι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 9.5:}} — _middle_, _aorist_ ἐπι-κρατησάμενοι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|variant in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 6.13}}:—_passive_, _future_ κρατήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.501 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}} and, with variant{κρατηθήσομαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9}}:—**to be strong, powerful**: hence,
**I** _absolutely_, **rule, hold sway**, Ἤλιδα.., ὅθι κρατέουσιν Ἐπειοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.275, 15.298}}; μέγα κρατέων ἤνασσε with mighty **sway**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.172; ἅπας δὲ τραχύς, ὅστις ἂν νέον κρατῇ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 35}}; ὁ κρατῶν **the ruler**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 951, 1664, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 738, etc.; θῶπτε τὸν κρατοῦντ᾽ ἀεί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 937; οἱ κρατοῦντες [prev. author] “Ch.” 267, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 530, etc.; τὸ κρατοῦν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 133 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 714c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1255a15}}; ἡ κρατοῦσα **the lady of the house** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 734.}}
**I.2** in Poets, with _dative_, **rule among**, μέγα κρατέεις νεκύεσσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.485; ἀνδράσι καὶ θεοῖσι 16.265}}; Φθίᾳ **rule in** Phthia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.50; ἐν Ἰλιάδι χθονί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 4.}}
**I.3** with _genitive_, **to be lord** or **master of, rule over**, πάντων Ἀργείων, πάντων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.79, 288, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.274; Ὀλύμπου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 149 (Lyric poetry); δωμάτων [prev. author] “Ag.” 1673; ὅπλων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1337}}; κ. τοῦ βίου **to be master of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.137; αὑτοῦ κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1099, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.26, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 405; ἡδονῶν καὶ ἐπιθυμιῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 196c, etc.; τῶν πραγμάτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.27}}; τοῦ μὴ πείθεσθαι τοῖς νόμοις κρατῆσαι **to be above** obedience.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 4.6.}}
**II** **conquer, prevail, get the upper hand**, _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 324, etc.; πολλῷ ἐκράτησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.77; εἰ τὰ τοῦ Μήδου κρατήσειε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.62; ὁ μὴ πειθόμενος κρατεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 272b; ἔνθα τἀναιδὲς κρατεῖ 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 111: with _dative_ modοκυντ- 55.9, +others, 3rd c.BC)}}; πλοῖα; πάλᾳ, ἱπποδρομίᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.20, “I.” 3.13; μάχῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 612; ταῖς ναυσί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 648; τῷ Φοινίκων ναυτικῷ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.16; also θουρίῳ ἐν Ἄρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 614 (Lyric poetry); ἐν τοῖς πολέμοις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 184: c._accusative_ _cognate_, κ. στάδιον 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 6.15, compare 7; ὀκτὼ νίκας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Epigrammata - PLG” 1; τὸν ἀγῶνα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.18}}; τὴν μάχην variant for{τῇ μάχῃ} in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.30; τὴν πρεσβείαν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.6}}; πάντα **in** all things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1522}}; οἱ κρατοῦντες the **conquerors**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.26; τὰ κατὰ πόλεμον κρατούμενα τῶν κρατούντων εἶναί φασιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1255a7.}}
**II.b** **to be superior**, πλήθους ἕκατι ναυσὶν κρατῆσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 338}}: _absolutely_, **to be the best**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 2.7 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II.c** of reports, etc., **prevail, become current**, φάτις κρατεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 293, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 978; λόγος κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 738; νόμιμα δὲ τὰ Χαλκιδικὰ ἐκράτησεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.5; κρατεῖ ἡ φήμη παρά τισι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.26.11.}}
**II.2** c._infinitive_, **prevail so that**, κ. τῷ πλήθει ὥστε μὴ αὐτίκα τὰς πύλας ἀνοίγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.104: _impersonal_, κατθανεῖν κρατεῖ}} **'tis better to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1364; κρατεῖ μὴ γιγνώσκοντ᾽ ἀπολέσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 248 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.3** c._genitive_, **conquer, prevail over**, τῶν ἐναντίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 85, compare “OC” 646, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 955 (Lyric poetry), etc.; κ. τινὸς τὸν ἀγῶνα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 2.5: _metaphorically_, τό τοι νομισθὲν τῆς ἀληθείας κρατεῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 86}}; κ. τῆς διαβολῆς **get the better of** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.53}}; ὁ λόγος τοῦ ἔργου ἐκράτει **surpassed, went beyond** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69}}; ἡ φύσις.. τῶν διδαγμάτων κρατεῖ **is better than**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 213, compare 169.}}
**II.3.b** of food, **digest, assimilate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3, 14, Mnesith. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.54b, Phylotim.[prev. work] 3.79c}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.5.15; τῆς τροφῆς μὴ κρατηθείσης 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.654b.}}
**II.4** c._accusative_, **conquer, master**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.25, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 215, “Th.” 189, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 490, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1346, “Av.” 420, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.32, etc.}}; μάχῃ, τῷ πολέμῳ τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.2, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.30; τῷ λόγῳ τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 539}}; πάχει μάκει τε **in**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.245}}; **outdo**, τοὺς φίλους εὖ ποιῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 11.15; τῷ διαφθαρῆναι χρήμασιν ἢ μὴ κεκράτηκα Φίλιππον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.247}}; **surpass**, κρατεῖ δὲ ὁ τῆς ἡδονῆς [βίος] τὸν τῆς φρονήσεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12a}}:—_passive_, **to be overcome**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 750 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ὕπνῳ [prev. author] “Eu.” 148 (Lyric poetry); ὑπὸ τοῦ ὕπνου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. δ᾽; ὑπὸ τῶν ἡδονῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 633e.}}
**III** **become master of, get possession of**, τῆς ἀρχῆς, τῶν νεκρῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.92, 4.111; πολλὰ φρονέοντα μηδενὸς κ. [prev. author] 9.16; σέθεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 387; οὔπω ἡ βουλή σου ἐκράτει 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.26; κ. τῆς γῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.6; ναυσὶ τῆς θαλάσσης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 240a}}; κ. τῆς λέξεως **have** it **at command, remember** it, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.275b}}; **master** by the intellect, πάντων τῶν τῆς ἱστορίας μερῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.20.2}}:—_passive_, **to be mastered**, δεῖ ἐν ταῖς τέχναις καὶ ἐπιστήμαις ταῦτα κρατεῖσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331b38, compare “Po.” 1456a10 (probably to be read instead of κροτεῖσθαι).}}
**IV** **lay hold of**, τῆς χειρός {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.19.16, NT.Matt.9.25, NT.Mark.9.27.}}
**IV.2** c._accusative_ _of things_, **seize, win and keep**, especiallyby force, πᾶσαν αἶαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 255; θρόνους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1381}}; **seize, hold fast, arrest**, τινα {{NT+2nd c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 63, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.18.8, NT.Matt.14.3; τένοντα Batrachomyomachia 233; τὰς χεῖράς τινος “PLips.” 40iii2 (4th-5th c.AD)}}; **secure, grasp**, τὴν ἀκατονόμαστον Τριάσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Zos.Alch. p.230 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**IV.3** **hold up, support**, τινα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.38}}; **maintain** a military post, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.7}}; **hold fast**, τὰς παραδόσεις {{NT|NT.2Thes.2.15}}; **keep, retain,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 61 (b).229 (2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, οὐκ ἦν δυνατὸν κρατεῖσθαι αὐτὸν ὑπ᾽ αὐτοῦ (i.e. τοῦ θανάτου) {{NT|NT.Act.2.24}}; ἡ κτῆσις τοῖς τέκνοις κεκράτηται **has been reserved** for, **settled** upon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 viii 36 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV.4** in Law, **possess a title to**, κ. καὶ κυριεύειν c._genitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 319.19 (3rd c.AD), etclass=bibl [prev. author] “Pl.” 536}}: _absolutely_, **tumult, uproar**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 880:_passive_, Egypt, 1st c.AD), “BGU” 742 iii 6 (_passive_, 2nd c.AD).}}
**IV.5** **hold in the hand**, ὁ κρατῶν τοὺς ἑπτὰ ἀδτέρας ἐν τῇ δεξιᾷ αὐτοῦ {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rev.2.1; πόαν 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.93; ἄρτον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.99d; σκῆπτρον 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.289c, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 44, 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.6}}, etc.; δακτύλιον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PMag.Lond” 46.451(4th c.AD).}}
**IV.6** **endure, put up with**, τὸν ἀργυροπράτην {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1844 (6th c.AD).}}
**V** **control, command**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 282:}} —_passive_, αἰσχρὰ τῷ νόμῳ κρατούμενα **controlled** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 755; κρατεῖσθαι ὑπὸ τοῦ προβουλεύματος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.52; διαθέσει 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 27.}}
**VI** **repair, make good**, τὸ βεδὲκ (Hebrew) τοῦ οἴκου {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.12.5.}}

2904:
** κράτος** [ᾰ],
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **κάρτος**, εος, τό, both in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus; _Aeolic dialect_}} **κρέτος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 25}}:— **strength, might**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} especially of **bodily strength**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἔπεφνε δόλῳ, οὔ τι κράτεΐ γε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.142; ἔχει ἥβης ἄνθος, ὅ τε κ. ἐστὶ μέγιστον 13.484}}, etc.; τὸ γὰρ αὖτε σιδήρου γε κ. ἐστίν this (i.e. τὸ βάψαι) is what gives **strength** to iron, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.393: generally, δικαία γλῶσσ᾽ ἔχει κ. μέγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 80; μηχανῆς ἔστω κ. Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Supplices” 207}}]; κατὰ κράτος with **all one's might** or **strength**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|πολιορκεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.64; πολεμεῖν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 692d; ἐξελέγχεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.20}}], etc.: frequently in phrase αἱρεῖν κατὰ κ. take **by storm**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.100, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.119, etc.; also ἀνὰ κράτος διώκειν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.23; ἐλαύνειν [prev\. author] “An.” 1.8.1, etc.; ἀπὸ κράτους 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.34}}]; πρὸς ἰσχύος κράτος, opposed to λόγῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” 594.}}
**2** personified, K. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Βία τε Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 12; K. καὶ Δίκη [prev\. author] “Ch.” 244}}].
**II** **power**, τοῦ γὰρ κ. ἐστὶ μέγιστον, of Zeus, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.118, etc.; τοῦ γὰρ κ. ἔστ᾽ ἐνὶ οἴκῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.359, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.214; Ζηνὸς κ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.96, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.” 527 (Lyric poetry); ἐκπίπτειν κράτους, of Zeus, [prev. work] 948; τὸ κ. τοῦ θεοῦ Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Psa.61(62).11}}, etc.: _plural_, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ὑποχείριος κράτεσιν ἀρσένων Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Supplices” 393 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ant.” 485}}]; especially of political power, **rule, sovereignty**, [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|ὁ μαιόμενος τὸ μέγα κρέτος ὀντρέψει τάχα τὰν πόλιν Alcaeus Lyricus [{7-6th c.BC}] [same place]; τὸ κ. περιθεῖναί τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.129; ἐς τὸ πλῆθος φέρειν τὸ κ. [prev\. author] 3.81}}]; τὸ πᾶν κ. ἔχειν to be all-**powerful**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 7.3; ἀρχὴ καὶ κ. τυραννικόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 373}}; βασιλεὺς πρῶτος ἐν κράτει Ὀδρυσῶν ἐγένετο in **real power**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; later τὸ κ. τῶν Ῥωμαίων “POxy.” 41i2 (3rd-4th c.AD): in _plural_, κράτη καὶ θρόνους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 173, compare “OT” 586, etc.; θρόνων κράτη}} sovereign **power**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Ant.” 166.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, **power over**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τὸ Περσέων κ. ἔχοντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.69; τὸ κ. εἶχε τῆς στρατιῆς [prev\. author] 9.42; πᾶν κ. ἔχων χθονός Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Supplices” 425 (Lyric poetry); τῶν ἄλλων δαιμόνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 949; δὸς κ. τῶν σῶν δόμων Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Choephori” 480; δωμάτων ἔχειν κ. Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Thesmophoriazusae” 871; τὸ τῆς θαλάσσης κ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.143; μετὰ κράτους τῆς γῆς [prev\. author] 8.24}}]; ὧν ἂν ᾖ τὸ κ. τῆς γῆς whoever have **possession** of the land, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 4.98; κ. ἔχειν ἑαυτοῦ Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Politicus” 273a}}]: _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀστραπᾶν κράτη νέμων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 201}} (Lyric poetry).
**II.3** of persons, **a power, an authority**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Ἀχαιῶν δίθρονον κ. Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Agamemnon” 109 (Lyric poetry), compare 619, “Th.” 127 (Lyric poetry).}}]
**III** **mastery, victory**, frequently in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.509, 6.387, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.280; κ. ἄρνυσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 838 (Lyric poetry); νίκη καὶ κράτη Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Supplices” 951}}]; ἀέθλων κ. **victory in**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).4; νίκη καὶ κ. τῶν δρωμένων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 85}}; κ. ἀριστείας **the meed** of highest valour, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Aj.” 443; νίκη καὶ κ. πολεμίων Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 962a; κ. πολέμου καὶ νίκη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.130}}].
**IV** in Medicine texts, in _plural_, **ligaments**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mul.” 2.167.}}
**IV.2** ={ταρσός}, **back of the hand**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.144.}}
**V** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **ten,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 59}}.—This word and its derivations take two forms, κρατ- and καρτ-; the latter is mostly _Epic dialect_, as κάρτος, κάρτιστος, καρτύνω, but in κρατερός and καρτερός the reverse holds, see at {κρατερός} near the end; κρατέω, κρατύς have no form καρτ-. (κρατ- and καρτ- from kṛt-, weak form of κρετ-, compare κρέτος, κρέσσων.)

2907:
** κρέας**, τό,
_Doric dialect_ **κρῆς** (which see), _Epic dialect_ **κρεῖας** uncertain conjecture in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Ananius Lyricus 5.3; _Attic dialect_ _genitive_ κρέως 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 728}}; Cretan dialect {{Refs|κρίως “GDI” 5128 (from Vaxos): _plural_ κρέα “IG” 12.84.26}}, etc.; _genitive_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|κρεῶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.98, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73, “IG” 12.10.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.” 191, etc.; _Epic dialect_ κρειῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.551}}, +others, κρεάων [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Hymnus ad Mamercus Elegiacus” 130; _dative_ κρέασι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.311}}, κρέεσσι Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.47, κρεάεσσι Epic.in “Arch.Papyrus” 7.4. [κρέᾰ 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyc.” 126, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “V.” 363, +others, κρέ) elided 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.65, 470, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Th.” 558, κρέᾱ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 20}} (assuming variant).]:—**flesh, meat**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.477, etc.; ἄρνειον κ.}} **piece** of lamb, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 45, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pl.” 1137; ἐρίφειον 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 222.6; τρία κρέα [ἢ] καὶ πλείω Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.2; τέτταρα.. κρέα μικρά 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 172.3}}] (_anapaest meter_): _plural_, mostly in _collective_ sense, **dressed meat**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.65, etc.; κ. ἑφθά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.23}}; κ. ἀνάβραστα, ὠπτημένα, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.” 553, “Pl.” 894; κ. ὀρνίθεια [prev\. author] “Nu.” 339; βοῶν [prev\. author] “Pax” 1280; βόεια Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 338c; δαῖτα παιδείων κρεῶν Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Agamemnon” 1242, 1593}}]; κ. Ἀθηναίοις μερίζειν, νέμειν τῷ δήμῳ, {{Refs|“IG” 22.334.15, 24.}}
**2** **carcass**: hence, **body, person**, τοῦδε τοῦ κρέως (i.e. ἐμοῦ) S. [prev. cited] (satyric): in Comedy texts addresses, like{κάρα}, ὦ δεξιώτατον κρέας [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.” 421, compare 457: proverbial, ὁ λαγὼς τὸν περὶ τῶν κρεῶν [δρόμον] τρέχει 'to save one's bacon', 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 4.85, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1087b; so νεναυμάχηκε τὴν περὶ τῶν κρεῶν Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Ranae” 191}}], see Scholia (κρεϝας, cf. Sanskrit _krauís_ 'raw meat', Latin _cruor._)

2910:
Included with: ** κρεμ-άννῡμι**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 830b}}, etc.; **κρεμαννύω**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 612a10, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “CP” 4.3.3}}; **κρεμάω**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mir.” 831a8, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “NA” 5.3}}, etc.; **κρεμνάω**, {{Refs|Demetrius Astrologus “Eloc.” 216, “Gp.” 4.15.15}}; **κρεμάζω**, {{LXX|LXX.Job.26.7}}(variant{κρεμνῶν}): _present_ _participle_ **κρεμάντες** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.25d}}: _future_ κρεμάσω [ᾰ] {{LXX+8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Comicus 8, LXX.Gen.40.19; _Attic dialect_ κρεμῶ, ᾷς, ᾷ, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pl.” 312 (Lyric poetry); _Epic dialect_ κρεμόω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.83}}: _aorist 1_ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐκρέμᾰσα Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Thesmophoriazusae” 1028}}], _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry {{Refs 8th c.BC+|κρέμασα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.67, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.192; _Doric dialect_}} _infinitive_ {{Refs|κραμάσαι “IG” 42(1).122.3}} (Epid.); _perfect_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|κεκρέμᾰκα 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 17}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ _infinitive_ [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|κρεμάσασθαι {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 629}}], _subjunctive_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|ἐκ-κρεμάσωμαι “AP” 5.91 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus):}} — _passive_, **κρέμαμαι**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Av.” 1387 (also κρεμᾶται “Anacreontea” 16.17)}}; _infinitive_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κρέμασθαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10, “Acut.” 30, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 74.4}}; _subjunctive_ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|κρέμωμαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Art.” 70, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh.” 1415a13; _optative_ κρεμαίμην Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Acharnenses” 945, “V.” 298, “Nu.” 870}}]: _imperfect_ ἐκρεμάμην, ω, ατο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.21}}, etc.: _future_ κρεμήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ach.” 279, “V.” 808, “PCair.Zen.” 202.9 (3rd c.BC)}}: _aorist_ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐκρεμάσθην Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Thesmophoriazusae” 1053}}], etc.: _perfect_ _imperative_ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|κεκρεμάσθω 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 181.7, variant in 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Quadr.” 13}}: _pluperfect_ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|κατα-κεκρέμαστο 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.26}}. (Cf. κρημνός, Goth. _hramjan_ 'crucify'):
**I** **hang up**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|σειρὴν.. ἐξ οὐρανόθεν κρεμάσαντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.19; τόξον ἐκ πίτυος Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “fragment” 251; ἀπὸ κάλω κ. σαυτόν Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Ranae” 122}}]; καὶ κρεμόω προτὶ νηόν will bring them to the temple and **hang them up** there as an offering, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.83; κ. τινὰ τῶν ὄρχεων Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Plutus” 312}}]; κ. [τὰς ὗς] τῶν ὀπισθίων σκελῶν **by** the hind legs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 632a23; κρεμάσας τὸ νόημα, in allusion to Socrates in his basket, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” 229, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 126.17; κρεμάσαι τὴν ἀσπίδα}} **hang up** one's shield, i.e. have done with war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ach.” 58; τὴν πανοπλίαν [prev\. author] “Av.” 436}}:—so in _middle_, πηδάλιον κρεμάσασθαι **hang up one's** rudder, i.e. give up the sea, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 629.}}
**I.2** **hang**, τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pol.” 1311b39, “Oec.” 1352a11}}; **crucify**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caes.” 2, etc.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be hung up, suspended**, ὅτε τ᾽ ἐκρέμω (_2nd pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_) ὑψόθεν when **thou wert hanging**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.18, compare 21; μηδ᾽ ὁ Ταντάλου λίθος τῆσδ᾽ ὑπὲρ νήσου κρεμάσθω 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 53}}; **to be hung up** as a votive offering, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.34, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, 66, etc.; τὰ σπλάγχνα οἱ δοκέει κρέμασθαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10; κάτω κρέμανται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 431; κρεμήσεται.. ἐπὶ τοῦ παττάλου Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Vespae” 808; κ. ἐφ᾽ ἵππων Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 3.2.19; ἐκ ποδῶν κατωκάρα κ. Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Acharnenses” 945; αἱ μέλιτται κ. ἐξ ἀλλήλων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 627b13}}]: _metaphorically_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀμφὶ φρασὶν ἀμπλακίαι κρέμανται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.25}}; μῶμος κρέματαί τινι censure **hangs over** him, {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.74; δόλιος αἰὼν ἐπ᾽ ἀνδράσι κ. [prev\. author] “I.” 8(7).14}}; κρέμασθαι ἔκ τινος **to be wholly taken up with** a thing, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 831c; ὁ ἐκ τοῦ σώματος κρεμάμενος Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 8.19}}].
**II.2** **to be hanged**, of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hipp.” 1252, 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 9.10, “PCair.Zen.” [prev. cited]}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **to be in suspense**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|ἵνα μὴ κρέμηται ἡ διάνοια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1415a13}}; κ. [ὁλόγος] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).754.}}
**II.4** ={ὀκλάζω}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 65 (ubi see Scholia).}}

2911:
** κρημνός**, ὁ,
irregular
**A**_plural_ **κρημνά**, τά, variant for{κρημνούς} in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus}} Mynd.63:—**overhanging bank**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”)}} of the **bank** of a river, **edge** of a trench, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|12.54, 21.175, 234, 244, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.22; κ. θαλάσσας [prev\. author] “fragment” 201; κ. μαλακοί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 615b31}}; later, **beetling cliff, crag**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀπὸ τοῦ κ. ὠθέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.103}}; ἀναθεῖναι ἐπὶ κρημνόν τιν᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pl.” 69; κατὰ τῶν κ. ἅλλεσθαι}} down from the **cliffs** of Epipolae, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.45; κατὰ κ. ῥιφέντες Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 944a}}]; οἱ K., the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Screes”, on the Sea of Azof, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.20, 110.}}
**A.2** in _plural_, **edges** of an ulcer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Loc.Hom.” 29.}}
**A.3** **labia pudendi**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 47, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.174, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “Onom.” 112.}}

2915:
** κρῑθ-ή**, ἡ,
mostly in _plural_, **barleycorns, barley** (compare κρῖ), the meal being ἄλφιτα: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|πυρῶν ἢ κριθῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.69, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.110, 19.112, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.” 1101; κριθᾶν μέδιμμνον “IG” 42(1).40.7 (Epid.); τὰς οὐλοχύτας φέρε δεῦρο—τοῦτο δ᾽ ἐστὶ τ;—κριθαί Strato Comedy texts 1.35}}; οἶνος ἐκ κριθέων πεποιημένος a kind of **beer**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.77; ἐκ κριθῶν μέθυ Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Supplices” 953, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “fragment” 106; κριθαὶ πεφρυγμέναι, ={κάχρυς}, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.22, cf. Moer. p.213 P.: _plural_}}], also of **species of barley**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 8.1.1: _sing._, “PGrenf.” 2.29.9 (2nd c.BC); κ. Ἰνδική}} **millet, Sorghum halepense**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 8.4.2.}}
**II** **pustule on the eyelid, stye**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Epid.” 2.2.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.742.}}
**III** **barley-corn, the smallest weight**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Lap.” 46.}}
**IV** in _singular_, ={πόσθη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 965. (The connexion with Latin _hordeum_, OHG. _gersta_ is doubtful.)}}

2917:
** κρίμα**, ατος, τό,
(κρίνω) **decision, judgement**, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.58, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.1.12, LXX.Psa.118(119).7, +others, “NT.Rom.11.33”, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epict.” 2.15.8; περί τινος 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.47}}; **verdict** on a literary work, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.” 5.23.}}
**2** **decree, resolution**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|δήμου 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.12; ἱερομνημόνων “BCH” 27.107, compare “IGRom.” 3.58,66 (from Prusias).}}
**3** **legal decision,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.56 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 826 E ii 29 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; **decision** of arbitrators, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 421.44 (Thermum, 3rd c.BC)}}; especially **sentence, condemnation**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.21.22, +others, “NT.Mark.12.40”, etc.}}
**II** **matter for judgement, question**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|οὐκ εὔκριτον τὸ κρῖμα Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Supplices” 397}}].
**II.2** **law-suit**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.18.22, NT.1Cor.6.7.}}
**III** ={κρίσις}, **judging, judgement,** NT.John.9.39, {{NT|NT.Acts.24.25}}, etc. [ῖ in A. [prev. cited] (nisi to be read κρεῖμα): ῐ in post-classical poetry; frequently written κρίμα in codices]

2918:
** κρίνον** [ῐ], τό,
irregular _plural_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κρίνεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92}}; _dative_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κρίνεσιν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 98, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” 911}}, etc.:—**white lily, Lilium candidum**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 6.6.8, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.56, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.27, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.102; κ. πορφυροῦν}} **Turk's cap lily, L. chalcedonicum**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 6.6.3, cf. Dsc.[same place]: proverbial, κρίνου γυμνότερος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.181c}}: hence, of a needy man, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.197, etc.: symbolic of death, see at {κολοκύντη}.}}]
**2** **Egyptian bean, Nelumbium speciosum**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92.}}
**II** kind of **choral dance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Apollophanes Comicus 2.}}
**III** kind of **loaf**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.114f.}}
**IV** **architectural ornament,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).161 A 72 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}

2920:
** κρίσις** [ῐς], εως, ἡ,
(κρίνω) **separating, distinguishing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τοῦ πλέω καὶ τοῦ μὴ πλέω 5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 7}}; τῶν ὁμοιογενῶν, τῶν διαφερόντων, uncertain reading in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “EN” 1165a34.}}
**2** **decision, judgement**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|περὶ τούτων Parmenides Poeta Philosophus [{4-5th c.BC}] 8.15; τὴν Κροίσου κ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34; ἐν θεῶν κρίσει Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Agamemnon” 1289; κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ κ. ἐμήν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ὅρκος” 1}}]; κ. οὐκ ἀληθής no certain **means of judging**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” 501 (Lyric poetry); πολίτης ὁρίζεται τῷ μετέχειν κρίσεως καὶ ἀρχῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275a23; κρίσεως προσδεόμενα Epicurus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 32 G., compare “Herc.” 1420.3; αἱ τῶν πολλῶν κ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.75}}] K.; Κρίσις, title of a play by Sophocles on the Judgemént of Paris; κ. τινός **judgement on** or **respecting**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τῶν μνηστήρων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.131; ἀέθλων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.21, “N.” 10.23; μορφῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 26}}: ἡ τῶν ὅπλων κ., referring to the story of Ajax, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” 620b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Po.” 1459b5; κρίσιν.. τοῦ βίου πέρι ὧν λέγομεν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 360e; κ. ἀμφ᾽ ἀέθλοις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.80; κ. διημαρτημένη “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 1.50}}]; κ. συνετή {{Refs|Cleanth.[prev. work] 128}}; **power of judgement**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|κρίσει πραγμάτων διαφέρεσθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.14.10}}; κατὰ κρίσιν with **judgement, advisedly**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 6.11.8.}}
**3** **choice, election**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|κ. ποιεῖσθαι τῶν ἀξίων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321a30, compare 1271a10.}}
**4** **interpretation** of dreams or portents, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Dan.2.36, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.116, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “AJ” 2.5.7.}}
**II** **judgement** of a court, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|οὐδὲν ἂν τῆς ὑμετέρας κ. ἔδει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.2}}; **trial, suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|προκληθέντας ἐς κρίσιν περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.34}}; καθιστάναι ἑαυτὸν ἐς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 131; κρίσιν ποιεῖν τινι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.35}}; κρίσεως τυχεῖν to be put on one's **trial**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.” 249a; εἰς κ. ἄγειν [prev\. author] “Lg.” 856c}}; ἡ κ. γίγνεταί τινι [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. passage]; κρίσιν ὑποσχεῖν [prev. work] 871d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.125; τὰς κρίσεις ποιεῖσθαι περί τινος Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 4.40, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77; τὰς κ. διαδικάζειν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 876b}}]; κρίσιν λελογχότα Μειδίᾳ ἐξούλης Test. cited in [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.82; αἱ κ. τῶν συμβολαίων Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] 2.447e}}].
**II.b** **result of a trial, condemnation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 1.6.5.}}
**II.c** ἡμέρα κρίσεως Day of **Judgement,** NT.Mat.10.15.
**II.2** **trial** of skill or strength, πρὸς τόξου κρίσιν in archery, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Tr.” 266; δρόμον.., οὗ πρώτη κ. [prev\. author] “El.” 684; κ. ποιεῖν ὁπότερος εἴη τὴν τέχνην σοφώτερος Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Ranae” 779; θεῶν ἔριν τε καὶ κ. Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 379e}}].
**II.3** **dispute**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.5, 7.26}}.
**III** **event, issue**, κρίσιν σχεῖν to be **decided**, of a war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.29.5; κρίσεως τυχεῖν [prev\. author] 1.59.11; ἐν τοῖς πεπολιτευμένοις τὴν κ. εἶναι νομίζω 1}} suppose the **issue** depends upon my public measures, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.57.}}
**III.2** **turning point** of a disease, **sudden change** for better or worse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “VM” 19 (_plural_), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.550, etc.; κ. ξύντομος ἐπὶ τὸ κάκιον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ κρισίων” 34, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).231.}}
**IV** middle of the spinal column, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.177.}}

2922:
** κρῐτ-ήριον**, τό,
**means for judging** or **trying, standard**, frequently of the mental faculties and senses, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἔχων αὐτῶν τὸ κ. ἐν αὑτῷ Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Theaetetus” 178b, compare “R.” 582a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.448b, etc.; τὸ αἰσθητήριον καὶ κ. τῶν.. χυμῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1068a3, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Ep.” 1p.5U. (_plural_), “Sent.” 24, +others; περὶ κριτηρίου, title of works by Epicurus (3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 10.27), Posidonius (1st c.AD: Diocles Epigrammaticus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.54), and Ptolemy: generally, χρόνον εἶναι μέτρον καὶ κ. τάχους}}] **measure, test**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.26, etc.}}
**2** **court of judgement, tribunal**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 767b; καθίζειν κ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.33.12, compare “PHib.” 1.29 (_a_) (3rd c.BC), NT.1Cor.6.2, “IG” 14.951.20, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.20.7, “POxy.” 2134.6 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2.b** **decision** of a tribunal, **judgement**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 826 K 9 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}

2923:
** κρῐτ-ής**, οῦ, ὁ,
_vocative_ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|κριτή 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 118}}: (κρίνω):—**judge, umpire**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supp.” 397, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.160, etc.; ἐν πέντε κριτῶν γούνασι κεῖται 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 229}}; κ. τῶν ἀληθῶν, opposed to δοξαστής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.94; κριταὶ ἀπὸ τοῦ ἴσου, opposed to ἀγωνισταί, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.37; τῶν.. λεγομένων μὴ κακοὺς κ. [prev\. author] 1.120; κ. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phlb.” 65a}}; at Athens, usually of **the judges in the poetic contests**, [{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ach.” 1224, “Nu.” 1115, “Av.” 445, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.21; rarely, ={δικαστής}, 4th c.BC: Demades Orator 3: so _metaphorically_ in 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.232; πάντα τὰ στοιχεῖα κριτὴν εἴληφε, i. e. each element has found favour with some philosopher, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de An.” 405b8, compare “Pol.” 1337a42; of the “Judges” of Israel, LXX.Judg.2.16, +others; κ. δοθείς, =}}] Latin _judex datus,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 1195.1 (2nd c.AD); ἐπίλεκτος κ., =}} **judex selectus**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 567.10 (Attalia, 2nd c.AD).}}
**2** κ. ἐνυπνίων **interpreter** of dreams, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pers.” 226.}}
**II** κριτάς· ὀδόντας, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; compare κραντήρ.}}

2924:
** κρῐτ-ῐκός**, ή, όν,
**able to discern, critical**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|δύναμις σύμφυτος κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 99b35; οὐκ ἔχει ῥῖνα κριτικὴν πρὸς τοὖψον 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 1.4; αἰσθήσεις κ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.8}} K.; τὸ κ. **the power of discerning**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de An.” 432a16; ἡ κριτική (i.e. τέχνη) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Plt.” 260c, etc.: with _genitive_, ἡ γεῦσις τῶν σχημάτων κριτικωτάτη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 442b17, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Sens.” 43, 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 2.7}}; of persons, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|τὸν ὅλως πεπαιδευμένον] περὶ πάντων ὡς εἰπεῖν κ. τινὰ νομίζομεν εἶναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 639a9}}: especially in language, **grammarian, scholar, literary** **critic**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ax.” 366e, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.” 5.24, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.10}}, etc.; of Crates, [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.490e, who distinguished κ. and γραμματικός, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “M.” 1.79; εἰ δύναταί τις εἶναι κ. καὶ γραμματικός, title of work by Galen (“Libr.Propr.” 17); but τῶν ὕστερον γραμματικῶν κληθέντων πρότερον δὲ κ. Dio Chrysostomus Sophista [{1-2nd c.AD}] 53.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus cited in 2nd-3rd c.AD: Clemens Alexandrinus Theologus “Strom.” 1.16.79; οἱ κ. τῶν λόγων Philostratus Sophista [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.1.14}}]; πρὸς τοὺς κ., title of work by Chrysippus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Stoic.” 2.9; ἡ κ., opposed to ἡ γραμματική, Taurisc. cited in 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “M.” 1.248, cf. Scholia “DT” p}}.3 H. _adverb_ -{{Refs 5th c.BC+|κῶς, ἔχειν τινός 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4 “Praef.”, compare 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus “Praef.” p.7 N., 3rd c.AD: Menander Rhetor p.391 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**2** **of** or **for judging**, ἀρχὴ κ. the office **of judges**, opposed to ἀρχὴ βουλευτική, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pol.” 1275b19.}}
**II** ={κρίσιμος},{ἑβδομάς} {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.45 (_superlative_), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.134f, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus}} 9.93, +others _adverb_ -{{Refs|κῶς [prev\. author] “UP” 17.2}}, +others

2925:
** κρούω**,
_future_ -σω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “El.” 180}}: _aorist_ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἔκρουσα Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 4.5.18, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “fragment” 201}}]: _perfect_ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|κέκρουκα {4-5th c.AD}: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 3.38, (ἐκ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.” 228e, (προς-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.206:}}] —_middle_, _aorist_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐκρουσάμην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.40}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἐκρούσθην 3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus “Καταστερισμοί” 32}}: _perfect_ κέκρουμαι (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 7.4.26, or -ουσμαι (ἀπο-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ach.” 459}}:—**strike, smite**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ῥυτῆρι κ. γλουτόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 501}}; κρούσας δὲ πλευρὰ [τῶν ἵππων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “fragment” 779.6; τὸν λυχνοῦχον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “fragment” 83; τοῖς ποσὶ τὴν γῆν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.5}}; also εἰς τὴν χεῖρα τοῖς δακτύλοις κ. **with** the fingers, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.16: _metaphorically_, κνῖσα κ. ῥινὸς ὑπεροχάς}} **tickles**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 3.3.}}
**2** **strike** one **against** another, **strike together**, κ. χεῖρας **clap** the hands, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supp.” 720; τὰ ὅπλα κρουόμενα πρὸς ἄλληλα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22; τὰς ἀσπίδας πρὸς τὰ δόρατα Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “An.”}}] [same place]: _metaphorically_, ἀλλήλων τοὺς λόγους τοῖς λόγοις ἐκρούομεν ἄν **would have knocked** their heads together, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” 154e.}}
**3** κ. πόδα (i.e. κ. τὴν γῆν τῷ ποδί), in dancing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “El.” [prev. cited] (Lyric poetry); ἴχνος ἐν γᾷ κ. [prev\. author] “IA” 1043}} (Lyric poetry).
**4** _metaphorically_ from **tapping** an earthen vessel, to try whether it rings sound (compare {{Refs|κροῦσις 2)}}: **examine, try, prove**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|κρούετε ἀπολαμβάνοντες τὸ καλόν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Hp.Ma.” 301b}}]; κἂν διαπειρώμενος κρούσῃς [τὸν κόλακα] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.64d.}}
**5** **strike** a stringed instrument with a plectron, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 183, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ly.” 209b}}: generally, **play** any instrument (see. κροῦμα, κρουματικός) {{Refs|αὐλεῖ.. κρούων ἰαστί “Comedy texts Adesp.” 415}}: with _dative_, κ. κρεμβάλοις, ={κρεμβαλίζειν}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.636d.}}
**6** κ. τὴν θύραν **knock at** the door **on the outside**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ec.” 317, 990 (with play on signification 8), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Smp.” 1.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 310b, 314d, etc.; κόπτειν is better _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 154; later κ. ἐπὶ τὴν θύραν Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Judg.19.22}}.
**7** κ. σταθμὸν ἑτερόζυγον, ={κρουσιμετρέω}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 15; ὡς μήτε κρούσῃς μήθ᾽ ὑπὲρ χεῖλος βάλῃς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 796; κρούων γε μὴν αὐτὰς ἐωνούμην 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 184}}.
**8** obscene meaning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“AB” 101, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ec.” 990; κ. πέπλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 328}}.
**9** _middle_, κρούεσθαι πρύμναν **back water**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.51, 54, 3.78; αἱ πρύμναν κρουόμεναι νῆες 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.17.7}} (also in _active_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.3.8); κ. ἐπὶ π. τὴν ναῦν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.119}}: hence κρούεσθαι τὸ πτερόν **fly backwards**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “NA” 3.13:}} —also in _active_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.18.}}
**10** κρούειν ἀκράτῳ, see at {πατάσσω} {{Refs|11.2. (Cf. Lith. _krùšti_ 'bruise', 'pound', Lett. _krausēt_ 'thresh'.)}}

2926:
** κρυπ-τή**, ἡ,
**crypt, vault**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 1.}}
**II** see at {κρυπτός} ad near the end

2928:
** κρύπ-τω**,
_Epic dialect_ Iterat. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|κρύπτασκε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.272, -εσκε “Hymnus in Cererem” 239}}: _future_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|κρύψω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.350}}, etc.: _aorist 1_ ἔκρυψα, _Epic dialect_ {{Refs|κρύψα 11.244}}: _perfect_ κέκρῠφα (συγ-) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 18:}} —_middle_, _future_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κρύψομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 474, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ba.” 955}}: _aorist_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐκρυψάμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 246}} (Lyric poetry), etc.:—_passive_, _future_ {{LXX+5th c.BC+|κρυφθήσομαι “Dialexeis [ca. 5th c.BC]” 2.4, κρῠβήσομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 543, LXX.Jer.39(32).27, κεκρύψομαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.36}}: _aorist_ ἐκρύφθην, _Epic dialect_ κρ-, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.405, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ba.” 955, ἐκρύβην [ῠ] NT.John.8.59, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 127, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.2.2, (κατ-) 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.47}}; _participle_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κρῠφείς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1145}}: _perfect_ [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|κέκρυμμαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.443, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.57, etc.; _Ionic dialect_ 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _plural_ κεκρύφαται {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 730, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mul.” 2.163}}]:—**hide, cover**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus with collateral notion of protection, κεφαλὰς.. κορύθεσσι κρύψαντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.373}}; ὁ δέ μιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|σάκεϊ κρύπτασκε φαεινῷ 8.272, compare 13.405 (_passive_); κ. με.. πόδα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 114}}; later, simply, **hide**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κ. φάος ὀμμάτων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.40}}; **cover**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|τινά τινι Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Eumenides” 461, etc.; ὑφ᾽ εἵματος κ. χεῖρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 343}}]:—_middle_, κάρα κρυψάμενος **having cloaked his** head, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” 246 (Lyric poetry); φύει τ᾽ ἄδηλα καὶ φανέντα κρύπτεται}} **hides in its own bosom**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 647; παῖδά μ᾽ ἐκρύψατο κρωσσός “IG” 14.1909}}:—_passive_, **hide oneself, lie hidden**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|οὐρανῷ κρύπτεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 606; δαλὸς κρύπτεται ἐς σποδιάν [prev\. author] “Cyc.” 615 (Lyric poetry); ὑφ᾽ εἵματος κρυφείς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1145}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κρύψει σὺ κρύψιν ἥν σε κρυφθῆναι χρεών 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ba.”}} [prev. cited]
**2** **cover in the earth, bury**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 138, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” 621 (_passive_); χθονί [prev. work] 1546 (_passive_); τάφῳ [prev\. author] “Ant.” 196}}; ἐν κατώρυχι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 774; κατὰ χθονός [prev. work] 25; ὑπὸ γᾶν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.81; γῇ κ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.130}} (_passive_), compare {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ant.” 946 (Lyric poetry, _passive_):}} —_passive_, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Τιτῆνες ὑπὸ ζόφῳ.. κεκρύφαται {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Th.” [same place]; ἐν βένθεσιν νᾶσον κεκρύφθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.”}}] [prev. cited]
**3** Astron., **occult**, {{Refs|Theo Sm. p.193 H., +others:}} —_passive_, of stars not seen in any part of the night, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|κεκρύφαται {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 386}}]; of the heliacal **setting** of stars, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Phas.” p.8 H.}}
**4** **conceal, keep secret**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|οὐδέν τοι ἐγὼ κρύψω ἔπος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.350, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Th.” 74, etc.; κ. τι ἔνθα μή τις ὄψεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 658, compare “Tr.” 903, “El.” 436:}} —_middle_, {{Refs|πᾶν σοι φράσω τἀληθές, οὐδὲ κρύψομαι [prev\. author] “Tr.” 474}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|τὸ μὲν φάσθαι, τὸ δὲ καὶ κεκρυμμένον εἶναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.443}}; φάρμακα κεκρ. **secret**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andr.” 32; κεκρ. νάπη}} **secret**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” 1398; κεκρ. παγίς Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 689}}]; κεκρ. σκευωρία **secret** intrigue, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 31 vi 14 (2nd c.BC); κρυπτόμενα πράσσεται}} **in secret**, opposed to ἐπὶ μαρτύρων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.8, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72.}}
**4.b** **connive at**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 825 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5** with _double_ _accusative_, **conceal** something from one, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|μή με κρύψῃ τοῦτο Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 625, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 957, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hec.” 570, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pl.” 26, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.7, etc.; so κ. τι πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 588}}].
**6** in Rhet., **argue so that the opponent is unwarily led to an adverse conclusion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Top.” 156a7.}}
**7** in Medicine texts, in _passive_, **to be suppressed**, of the menses or lochia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mul.” 1.36, 154, 2.163.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **lie hidden**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὰ μὲν.. ὄμματα βλέποντα, τὰ δὲ κρύπτοντα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1117}} (assuming variant); also {{Refs|κ. τινά _conceal oneself from_ Hymni Homerici 1.7}}.— (καλύπτω is simply **cover**; κεύθω _cover_ so that no trace of it can be seen; κρύπτω _keep covered_, especially for purposes of concealment.)

2930:
** κρύσταλλος**, ὁ,
(κρύος, κρυσταίνομαι) **ice**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.152, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.477, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 149; κρύσταλλος ἐπεπήγει οὐ βέβαιος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23}}; ὁ παῖς τὸν κρύσταλλον proverbial, of persons who cannot keep a thing, but do not wish to let it go, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 5.58.}}
**2** ={νάρκη}, **numbness, torpor**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “H.” 3.155.}}
**II** **rock-crystal**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 781, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.67, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “NA” 15.8, etc.: also _feminine_, “AP” 9.753 (4th-5th c.AD: Claudianus Epigrammaticus)}}: as _adjective_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|οἱ κ. λίθοι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.52}}.

2932:
** κτάομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κτέομαι**, only as variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.112}}: _future_ κτήσομαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 6.4, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 200, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 289, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 417a, etc. (in _passive_ sense, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.15, assuming variant); κεκτήσομαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1022, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 514, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467a (ἐκτήσομαι in “La.” 192e, and probably in 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 110.4):}}_aorist_ ἐκτησάμην, _Epic dialect_ κτ-, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.4, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 2.59, etc.}}: _perfect_ κέκτημαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 437, etc., ἔκτημαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.402, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 795, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.44, and sometimes in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (κεκτῄμεθα and ἐκτῆσθαι}} in following lines, {{Refs|“R.” 505b, ἐκτῆσθαι τοῦ κεκτῆσθαι ἕνεκα “Tht.” 198d)}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐκτέαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23}}; _subjunctive_ κέκτωμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936b; _optative_ κεκτῄμην, ῇτο, [prev. work] 731c, 742e, κεκτῴμην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 282}} codices: _pluperfect_ ἐκεκτήμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74, 4.41, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.17, etc.; poetry κεκτήμην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 404}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔκτηντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.108}}; _Attic dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐκτήμεθα _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.37}}: for _future_ and _aorist_ _passive_, see below{{Refs|111.}}
**I** _present_, _imperfect_, _future_, and _aorist_,
**I.1** **procure for oneself, get, acquire**, κτήμασι τέρπεσθαι τὰ γέρων ἐκτήσατο Πηλεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.400, etc.}}; [οἰκῆας] Od.[same place]; γῆν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eu.” [same place], compare “Pers.” 770}}; of horses, **win** (as a prize), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.52}}; κτήσασθαι βίον ἀπό τινος **to get one's** living from a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106}}; **win** favour, and the like, χάριν ἀπό τινος, ἔκ τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 471, “Ph.” 1370; παρά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.43; τὴν εὔνοιαν τὴν παρὰ τῶν Ἑλλήνων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.68}}; κ. φίλους, ἑταίρους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1360, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 804 (troch.); κτήσασθαι παῖδας ἐξ ὁμοσπόρου [prev. author] “IT” 696, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1499, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.105; παῖδας ἐς δόμους κτήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 491, compare “Supp.” 225; πολλάκις δοκεῖ τὸ φυλάξαι τἀγαθὰ τοῦ κτήσασθαι χαλεπώτερον εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.23.}}
**I.1.b** of consequences, **bring upon oneself**, αὑτῷ θάνατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 968}}; **incur**, θεᾶς ὀργήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 777; κακά [prev. author] “El.” 1004; ξυμφοράς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 543; ἔχθραν πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.42}}; δυσσέβειαν κ. **get** a name for impiety, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 924; κακὸν λόγον πρὸς ἀστῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 166, compare “IT” 676; ἐκ τῶν πόνων τὰς ἀρετὰς κ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123.}}
**I.1.c** κ. τινὰς πολεμίους **make** them so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.17; οὔ ποτ᾽ εὔνουν τὴν ἐμὴν κτήσῃ φρένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1281.}}
**I.2** **procure** or **get** for another, ἐμοὶ δ᾽ ἐκτήσατο κεῖνος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20. 265; μέγαν τέκνοις πλοῦτον ἐκτήσω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 755 (troch.), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.1.}}
**II** in _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ with _future_ κεκτήσομαι, **to have acquired**, i.e. **possess, hold** (opposed to χρῆσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 28o d), οὐδ᾽ ὅσα φασὶν Ἴλιον ἐκτῆσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.402, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260b; ὅπλα μὴ ἐκτῆσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 778; στρατὸν πλεῖστον ἐκτημένοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.161; κοινὸν ὄμμ᾽ ἐκτημέναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 795}}; φωνὴν βάρβαρον κεκτ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1051; κεκτ. τινὰ σύμμαχον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1343; κ. κάλλος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.8; ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 340e; τέχνην 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.6}}; ποίησιν **to be master of** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 829c: uncertain in}}_aorist_, ἀγορὰς κτησάμενοι **having** market-places, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153 (to be read στησάμενοι): with _impersonal_ subject, πραγμάτων ἀγῶνας κεκτημένων}} **involving** effort, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 21}}:—the differentbetween _present_ and _perfect_ appears from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.3, ἃ [χρήματα] καὶ κτωμένους εὐφραίνει καὶ κεκτημένους.. ποιεῖ ζῆν}}: later, _present_ in _perfect_ sense, {{NT|NT.Luke.18.12.}}
**II.b** of evils, ἄγος κεκτήσεται θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1022; κακά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 272; φθόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 870c.}}
**II.c** **have in store**, opposed to ἔχω, **have in hand**, ready for use, ἔχων τε καὶ κεκτημένος.. κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1278; ἔχειν τε καὶ κεκτῆσθαι τὸ ψεῦδος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 382b. compare “Tht.” 197b, 198d, “Cra.” 393b}}; κ. ἱμάτιον **own**, opposed to ἔχειν (wear), {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 197b.}}
**II.d** _absolutely_, **to be a property-owner**, τῶν ἐκτημένων ἐν τῇ χώρᾳ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 633.73 (Milet., 2nd c.BC), compare 888.15 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** ὁ κεκτημένος **owner, master** (especially of slaves), as _substantive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 4, etc.; οἱ κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 337}}; of a husband, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 715}}; ἡ κεκτημένη my **mistress**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 762, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1126, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 61, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 48.}}
**III** _aorist 1_ _passive_ ἐκτήθην in _passive_ sense, **to be gotten**, ἃ ἐκτήθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123, 2.36}}; **to be obtained as property**, δουλόσυνος πρὸς οἶκον κτηθεῖσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 449 (Lyric poetry), compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.27, etc.}}: _future_ κτηθήσομαι {{Refs|LXX “Je.” 39 (32). 43.}}(_active_ κτάω{{Refs 6th c.AD+|very late, “PLond.” 1.77 (6th c.AD).)}}

2933:
** κτῆμα**, ατος, τό,
(κτάομαι) **anything gotten, piece of property, possession**, _singular_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, μή νύ τι.. δόμων ἐκ κτῆμα φέρηται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.19; later ταύτας [γυναῖκας] ἐξείλεθ᾽ αὑτῷ κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 245; ἡδὺ κ. τῆς νίκης λαβεῖν [prev. author] “Ph.” 81, compare “OT” 549, “Ant.” 702, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 230, 703, etc.; κ. ἐς αἰεί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22; ὡς ἡδὺ καὶ μακάριον τὸ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 496c, etc.}}; of a slave, παλαιὸν οἴκων κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 49, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.6, “Vect.” 4.42; κ. ἔμψυχον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1253b32}}; of a calf, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.14.3; κ. πάντων ἐστὶ τιμιώτατον ἀνὴρ φίλος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24.}}
**2** frequently in _plural_, **possessions**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} of **heirlooms**, δόμοις ἐν κτήματα κεῖται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.382, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.127}}; also, of all kinds of **property**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, κ. δαρδάπτουσιν 14.92, compare 18.144, +others; διέλαχον.. κτημάτων παμπησίαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 817, etc.}}; Ἔοως ὃς ἐν κτήμασι πίπτεις who fallest upon **wealth**, i.e. on **the wealthy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 782 codices (Lyric poetry): sometimes, χρήματα καὶ κ.}} **property in** money and **chattels**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 728e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.28}}; ={κτήνη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 484c, “Phd.” 62b}}; opposed to ἀγροί, **personal** (opposed to real) **property**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.43}}; less frequently of **landed property**, κ. ἔχων ἐν Βοιωτίᾳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.41 (_sing._ as variant), 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.6.3}}: later frequently in _singular_, **estate, farm, field**, etc., {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Act.5.1, “BGU” 530.21 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.; ἀμπελικὸν κ. **vineyard,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PRyl. 157.4 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** in _plural_, **materials**, κ. πιλητά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 6.4, 7.22.}}

2934:
** κτῆνος**, εος, τό,
(κτάομαι) mostly in _plural_ κτήνεα, _contraction_ κτήνη, **flocks and herds,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Hymni Homerici 30.10, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, 2.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 109c, “PStrassb.” 98.9 (2nd c.BC), “SIG” 633.73 (Milet., 2nd c.BC)}}; κ. τὰ δημιοπληθῆ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 129 (Lyric poetry)}}; of beasts in general, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 29}}; opposed to ἄνθρωποι, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 57}}; of swine, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.4.14; ὑϊκὰ κ. “BGU” 757.20 (1st c.AD).}}
**2** in _singular_, a single **beast**, as **an ox** or **sheep**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.3}}; **horse** or **mule for riding,** {{NT|NT.Luke.10.34, NT.Act.23.24}}; of a domestic animal, opposed to θηρίον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 5.11. (Late _dative_ _plural_ κτῆσι “PFlor.” 258.6, etc.)}}

2936:
** κτίζω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 23.6, etc.}}: _future_ -ίσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1060}}: _aorist_ ἔκτῐσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.263, etc.; poetry ἔκτισσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.62, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 289 (Lyric poetry), κτίσσα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.216, κτίσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.89}}: _perfect_ ἔκτῐκα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 1.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 7.5, 15.13}}:— _middle_, _poetryaorist_ ἐκτίσσατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).25, “fragment” 1.4 (ἐκτής- codices)}}:— _passive_, _future_ κτισθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus “Chr.” 5.38, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.56}}: _aorist_ ἐκτίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔκτισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.46, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 45, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 360.9}}:—**people** a country, **build houses and cities in** it, κτίσσε δὲ Δαρδανίην Il.[same place]; κ. χώρην, νῆσον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.149, 3.49.}}
**2** of a city, **found, build**, Θήβης ἕδος ἔκτισαν Od.[same place],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.167, 168, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.4, “PCair.Zen.” 169 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἀποικίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 815}}:—_passive_, **to be founded**, Σμύρνην τὴν ἀπὸ Κολοφῶνος κτισθεῖσαν **founded by emigrants from** Colophon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16, compare 7.153, 8.62}}; μήτε ἄστεα μήτε τείχεα ἐκτισμένα no **fixed** cities or walls, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.46; - ομένη πόλις 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.155 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**3** κ. ἄλσος **plant** a grove, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.89}}; βωμόν **set up** an altar, {{Refs|[prev. author] “O.” 7.42}}; ἑορτάν, ἀγῶνα, **found, establish** it, {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.69, 10(11).25 (_middle_)}}; τὸν Κύρνον.. κτίσαι, ἥρων ἐόντα **establish** his worship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.167; δαῖτάς τινι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 484 (_passive_)}}; τάφον τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1101; αἵρεσιν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.77 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; σύνοδον “IG” 22.1343.12 (1st c.BC).}}
**4** **produce, create, bring into being**, γόνῳ τινά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 172 (Lyric poetry)}}; **bring about**, τελευτήν {{Refs|[prev. work] 140 (Lyric poetry),compare “Ch.” 441 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ τὴν φιλίην ἐκτικώς {{Refs|“Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota”}} [prev. cited]; of painters, δένδρεα.. καὶ ἀνέρας ἠδὲ γυναῖκας Emp.[same place]; ἵπποισι τὸν χαλινὸν κτίσας **having invented** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 715 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5** **make** so and so, ἐλεύθερον κ. τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1060; ἔνθεον κτίσας φρένα [prev. author] “Eu.” 17, compare 714; ποτανὰν εἴ σέ τις θεῶν κτίσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 620 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 289 (Lyric poetry).}}
**6** **perpetrate** a deed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 898}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _kséti_ 'reside', **k[snull]itís** (={κτίσις}) 'habitation'.)

2937:
** κτίσ-ις** [ῐ], εως, ἡ,
(κτίζω) **founding, settling**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.5; ἀποικιῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.190, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.1.4 (_plural_), etc.}}
**2** loosely, ={πρᾶξις}, κούφα κ. an easy **achievement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.83.}}
**3** **creation**, κ. κόσμου {{NT|NT.Rom.1.20; ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς κτίσεως NT.Mark.10.6, 13.19, etc.}}
**II** **created thing, creature**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Jdt.9.12, NT.Mark.16.15, NT.Rom.8.19, etc.: in _plural_, LXX.Tob.8.5.}}
**III** **authority created** or **ordained**, {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.13.}}

2938:
** κτίσ-μα**, ατος, τό,
**colony, foundation**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.77; Παρίων 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.5.5, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.59; Λακωνικὸν κ. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.3.6}}; also, of a temple, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.6.1}}: generally, **building**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PSI” 1.84.8 (_plural_, 4th-5th c.AD).}}
**2** ={κτίσις}{{LXX+NT|11, LXX.Wis.9.2 (_plural_), LXX.3Mac.5.11, NT.Jam.1.18.}}
**II** ={κτίσις}{{Refs|1.1, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1382.50.}}

2939:
** κτίσ-της**, ου, ὁ,
**founder**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 484, “OGI” 111.9 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 13}}; of Apollo as founder of Cyrene, {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1903.85: _plural_, Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.64}}; ὁ τῆς στοᾶς κ., i.e. Zeno, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.345c, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.120: _metaphorically_, ἰατρικῆς κ. “IG” 14.1759.}}
**2** **builder,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 2144.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** **restorer**, τῆς πατρίδος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 22.}}
**III** {{LXX|“Creator”, ὁ κ. ἁπάντων LXX.Sir.24.8, +others}}

2941:
** κῠβέρν-ησις**,
_Doric dialect_ **κῠβέρν-ᾱσις**, εως, ἡ, **steering, pilotage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 488b.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **government**, πολίων of cities, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.72 (_plural_), cf. NT.1Cor.12.28 (_plural_)}}; θεοῦ by a god, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.162a.}}

2942:
** κῠβερν-ήτης**
(_Aeolic dialect_ κυμερνήτης, which see), ου, ὁ, **steersman, pilot**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.43, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 770, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.164, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 837, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341c, etc.}}; **skipper** of Nileboat, ναύκληρος καὶ κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.39.6 (3rd c.BC),compare “PGiss.” 11 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **guide, governor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 880, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247c}}; as an official title, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 89 iii 1 (6th c.AD).}}

2944:
** κυκλ-όω**,
_future_ -ώσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 462}}: _perfect_ κεκύκλωκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.116.10}}:— _middle_, _future_ -ώσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.20}}: _aorist_ ἐκυκλωσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.18, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.72:}} —_passive_, _future_ κυκλωθήσομαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|(variant -ώσομαι) 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.24}}: _perfect_ κεκύκλωμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.32 (in middle sense (ἐγ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 395)}}: _aorist_ ἐκυκλώθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.20}}: (κύκλος):—**encircle, surround**, Ὠκεανὸς.. κυκλοῖ χθόνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1379 (Lyric poetry); πόλιν.. κυκλώσας Ἄρει φονίῳ [prev. author] “IA” 775 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὅταν κυκλώσωσι [τοὺς ἰχθῦς] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 533b27}}:—more frequently in _middle_, κυκλώσασθαί τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.157, 9.18, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.13; κ. αὐτοὺς ἐς μέσον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.10, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 121 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.1.14, etc.: such forms as κυκλοῦνται, ἐκυκλοῦντο, etc., may belong to κυκλόω or to κυκλέω, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.127, 7.81, etc.: _absolutely_, κυκλούμενοι}} **by an enveloping movement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.76}}:—_passive_, **to be surrounded**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 247, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.81:}} —joined with _middle_, εἰ οἱ κυκλούμενοι κυκλωθεῖεν X. [prev. cited]
**2** **go round**, τὸ θυσιαστήριον {{LXX|LXX.Psa.25(26).6}}:—_passive_, κυκλωθεὶς τὸν Ἀδρίαν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.25.}}
**II** **move in a circle, whirl round**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).72; οὕτω κυκλώσω δαλὸν ἐν φαεσφόρῳ Κύκλωπος ὄψει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 462; κ. ἀεὶ τὸ σῶμα 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 4; οἱ κυκλοῦντες [τὴν θάλασσαν] ἄνεμοι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.29.10; ἵετο κυκλώσας βαλιὰ πτερὰ θῆλυς ἀήτης Callias Epigrammaticus in “PSI” 9.1092.53, compare 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 92b Diehl: _metaphorically_, πολλοὺς λογισμοὺς ἡ πονηρία κυκλοῖ}} **revolves, agitates**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 378}}:—_middle_, **hurl**, βέλη {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 7.17:}} —_passive_ (or _middle_), **go in a circle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.20}}; **dance** or **whirl round**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 267, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 811: _metaphorically_, δίναις κυκλούμενον κέαρ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 997 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **form into a circle**, κ. τόξα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.82 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), compare 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 17.5; incorrectly, κ. τόξοιο νευρήν 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 68.5}}:—_passive_, **form a circle**, of a bow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1066}}; also [τάφρος] περὶ τὸ πεδίον κυκλωθεῖσα **being drawn in a circle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118d.}}
**IV** _absolutely_, κυκλώσατε ἐπὶ τὸν βασιλέα κύκλῳ {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.11.8}}; ἐκύκλωσα ἐγὼ καὶ ἡ καρδία μου τοῦ γνῶναι{{LXX|LXX.Eccl.7.26(25).}}
**V** ={λακκίζειν}, {ἀμπέλους} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 2.8.}}

2945:
Related to: ** κυκλ-έω**,
**wheel along**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only once, κυκλήσομεν ἐνθάδε νεκροὺς βουσὶ καὶ ἡμιόνοισιν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.332.}}
**2** **move round** or **in a circle**, ὁδοῖς κυκλῶν ἐμαυτὸν εἰς ἀναστροφήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 226}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀνδρὶ δυσμενεῖ βάσιν κυκλοῦντα, _metaphorically_, from dogs **questing about** for the scent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 19; σὸν πόδ᾽ ἐπὶ συννοίᾳ κυκλεῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 632, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1379}}; κ. πρόσωπον, ὄμμα, look **round**, look **about**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 364, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 958 (Lyric poetry)}}; ={κυκλεύω}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 4.}}
**3** **bring round, repeat**, τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 300a33.}}
**II** _middle_ and _passive_, **form a circle round, encompass, encircle**, μηνοειδὲς ποιήσαντες τῶν νεῶν ἐκυκλεῦντο ὡς περιλάβοιεν αὐτούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.16 (elsewhere κυκλόομαι)}}; ἴδεσθέ μ᾽ οἷον ἄρτι κῦμα.. κυκλεῖται **encompasses** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 353 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **go round and round, revolve**, τὴν αὐτὴν φορὰν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 617a; χρόνου.. κατ᾽ ἀριθμὸν κυκλουμένου [prev. author] “Ti.” 38a; οὑμὸς αἰεὶ πότμος ἐν πυκνῷ θεοῦ τροχῷ κυκλεῖται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 871; ὁ βίος ἀγαθοῖς τε καὶ κακοῖς κ. πάντα τὸν αἰῶνα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.59}}; δι᾽ ἀλλήλων αὐτοῖς -εῖται τὸ κακόν, of the vicious circle in disease, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.360.}}
**II.3** **assemble in knots**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.20, “Cyr.” 6.2.12.}}
**II.4** _metaphorically_, of sayings, etc., **to be current, pass from mouth to mouth**, τὸ κυκλούμενον παρὰ πᾶσιν ἔπος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.118c.}}
**III** _intransitive_ in _active_, **revolve, come round and round**, πολλαὶ κυκλοῦσι νύκτες ἡμέραι τ᾽ ἴσαι (but read κυκλοῦνται as L had originally) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1365; δελφῖνες.. πέριξ κυκλοῦντες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.16 of.}}

2946:
** κῠλ-ισμα**, ατος, τό,
**roll**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Eze.10.13, “Hippiatrica” 79, 117}}; κ. κανθάρου, ball of dung rolled by a beetle, {{Refs|“PMag.Berol.” 1.223.}}
**II** ={κυλίστρα}, {{Refs|“Hippiatrica” 8}}; variant for {κυλισμός}, {{NT|NT.2Pet.2.22.}}

2947:
** κῠλίω** [ῑ],
later form of κυλίνδω: (προσ-) κύλιε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 202, (ἀνα-) κυλῖον 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 116}}:—**roll along**, γαστέρας αἱμοβόρως ἐκύλιον, of serpents, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.18; κυλίουσιν [ἀλλήλους] ἐν τῷ πηλῷ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 6}}; λόγοις τοὺς ῥήτορας κ. **rolling** them **over,** {{Refs|Comica Adespota 294}} codices: frequently in later Gr., {{LXX|LXX.Josh.10.18, +others: _metaphorically_, ἐκ κισσηρεφέος κεφαλῆς εὔϋμνα κυλίων ῥήματα Callias Epigrammaticus “epigram” in “Berl.Sitzb.” 1912.548}}:—_passive_, **roll, whirl along**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 290a25, +others}}; of bees, **grovel**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “HA” 625b5; πρὸς τοῖς ἑαυτοῦ γόνασι κυλιομένην 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.39}}; κ. περὶ τὴν ἀγοράν **to be always loitering** there, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1319a29}}; **roll about**, in pantomime, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1461b31.}}
**2** **roll up**, ἣν κυλίουσι κόπρον (i.e. κάνθαροι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 552a17.}}

2948:
** κυλλ-ός**, ή, όν,
**club-footed and bandy-legged**, opposed to βλαισός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 53, compare 62}}; κ. πούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 53, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1379.}}
**2** generally, **deformed, contracted**, κ. οὖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 40}}; **crippled in the arm**, κ. ἠκόντιζεν ἀμείνονα {{NT+1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.84 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), cf. NT.Matt.15.30, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 1.17, +others}}; ἔμβαλε κυλλῇ (i.e. χειρί) put into **a crooked hand**, i.e. **with** the fingers **crooked** like a beggar's, to catch an alms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1083, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])}}
**2** of things, **crooked**, κ. κυκλάς {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.776.10 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** κυλλά, τά, **choliambi**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 8.79.}}

2949:
** κῦμα**, ατος, τό,
(κύω) **anything swollen** (as if **pregnant**): hence,
**I** **wave, billow**, of rivers as well as the sea, in _singular_ and _plural_; κ. θαλάσσης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.209, +others; κ. ῥόοιο 21.263; κ. διϊπετέος ποταμοῖο [prev. work] 268, 326}}; κύματ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἠϊόνος κλύζεσκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.61; κύματ᾽ εὐρέϊ πόντῳ βάντ᾽ ἐπιόντα τε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 114 (Lyric poetry): less frequently in Prose, κύματος ἐπαναχώρησις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.89: collectively, ὡς τὸ κ. ἔστρωτο}} when the **swell** abated, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.193, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 344b35, +others}}
**I.2** _metaphorically_, **flood** of men, κ. χερσαῖον στρατοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 64, compare 114 (Lyric poetry), 1083 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**I.2.b** of **the waves of adversity**, etc., κ. ἄτης, κακῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 886 (_anapaest meter_), “Th.” 758 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 927; συμφορᾶς [prev. author] “Hipp.” 824}}; κελαινοῦ κ. μένος, of passion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 832; κ. κατακλυσμὸν φέρον νόσων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740e.}}
**I.2.c** phrases: μάτην με κῦμ᾽ ὅπως παρηγορῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1001; πρὸς κῦμα λακτίζειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1396; ἐκ κυμάτων.. γαλήν᾽ ὁρῶ [prev. author] “Or.” 279; ἐπ᾽ ῃόνι κύματα μετρεῖν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.60; ἀριθμεῖν τὰ κύματα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 84.}}
**I.3** Architecture texts, **waved moulding, cyma**, Λέσβιον κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 78.}}
**II** from κύω (as κύημα from κυέω), **foetus, embryo**, νεόσπορον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eu.” 659; γέμουσαν κύματος θεοσπόρου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 106}}; of the earth, κ. λαμβάνειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 128; δισσὸν κῦμ᾽ ἐλόχευσε τέκνων “Anthologia Graeca” 6.200 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** **young sprout** of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.6.9}}; especially of a cabbage, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.642.}}

2955:
** κύπτω**,
_future_ κύψω {{LXX|LXX.Psa.9.31 (10.10):}}_aorist_ ἔκυψα (see. below): _perfect_ κέκῡφα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 217}}:—**bend forward, stoop**, πλευρά, τά οἱ κύψαντι παρ᾽ ἀσπίδος ἐξεφαάνθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.468; ἔλαβεν.. κύψας ἐκ πεδίοιο 17.621, compare 21.69}}; ὁσσάκι γὰρ κύψει᾽ ὁ γέρων πιέειν μενεαίνων κτλ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.585; κ. ἐστὴν γῆν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14; κάτω κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 279 (Lyric poetry), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 24.8; κεκυφότες εἰς γῆν καὶ εἰς τραπέζας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 586a; χαμᾶζε 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 45: frequently in}}_aorist_ _participle_ with another Verb, ἔθει κύψας ran **with the head down**, i.e. **at full speed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1091 (_anapaest meter_); ὁμόσ᾽ εἶμι κύψας [prev. author] “Ec.” 863; ἐς τὴν γῆν κύψασα κάτω βαδίζει [prev. author] “fragment” 395}}; κύψας ἐσθίει eats **stooping**, i.e. **greedily**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 33; obscene meaning, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 22 Diehl.}}
**2** **hang the head from shame**, οὗτος, τί κύπτει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1354, “Th.” 930; or sorrow, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 30.6, 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 1.27, or thought, 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.21, 23 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**3** **bow down under a burden**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.323.}}
**4** κύψαι, = {ἀπάγξασθαι}, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 35, compare 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}
**5** of animals, **to be bowed forward**, opposed to the erect figure of man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 657a15}}; κέρεα κεκυφότα ἐς τὸ ἔμπροσθε horns **bent** forward, of certain African oxen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.183; ἐπὴν ὁ στόμαχος [τῆς ὑστέρης] ἐς τὸν ἀρχὸν κεκύφῃ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus [same place]}}

2960:
** κῡρι-ακός**, ή, όν,
(κύριος) **of** or **for an owner** or **master,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Stud.Pal. 22.177.18 (2nd c.AD)}}; but usually **of the Roman Emperor**, ὁ κ. φίσκος **the fiscus**, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2827 (Aphrod.), “Supp.epigram” 2.567 (Caria (?))}}; κ. ψῆφοι, λόγος, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.13, 18 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; κ. χρῆμα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 474.41 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** especially **belonging to the Lord (Christ**): K. δεῖπνον **the Lord's Supper**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.11.20}}; ἡ K. ἡμέρα **the Lord's day,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 1.10}}; τὸ Κυριακόν (i.e. δῶμα) **the Lord's house**, Edict.Maximiniap.{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 9.10.}}
**III** _substantive_ κυριακός, ὁ, spirit invoked in magic, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.916.}}

2961:
** κῡρι-εύω**,
**to be lord** or **master of**, πάντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.22, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1160b35; τῆς Ἀσίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.11; μυρίων γῆς πήχεων 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1099, compare “PEleph.” 14.14 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; τῶν γενημάτων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 105.47 (2nd c.BC)}}; τῆς θαλάττης {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 5; ὧν ἁ πόλις.. κυριεύει “IG” 5(2).510.4 (4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 2nd c.BC)}}; κυριεύειν τὴν γυναῖκα τοῦ ἀνδρός {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.27; σανίδων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 24; νεκρῶν καὶ ζώντων NT.Rom.14.9; κρατεῖν καὶ κ. “PStrassb.” 14.22 (3rd c.AD)}}; **gain possession of, seize**, ζωγρίᾳ τινῶν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.7.11, +others, compare 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 80.41: later with _accusative_, τὰ σώματα καὶ τὴν βοῦν κ. “PGrenf.” 1.21.13 (2nd c.BC)}}; τοῦ κυριεύοντος τὴν ὅλην οἰκουμένην {{Refs|“PMag.Lond.” 121.838}}: _absolutely_, **to be dominant**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.244}}:—_passive_, **to be dominated, possessed**, ὑπό τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 838a10.}}
**b** Astrology texts, of planets, κ. τοῦ σχήματος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 169, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 63.23}}:—_passive_, οἱ -όμενοι τόποι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 112.}}
**2** **to have legal power** to do, with _infinitive_, Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.35.}}
**II** ὁ κυριεύων (i.e. λόγος, which is expressed in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.19.1), a logical puzzle, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.615a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 22, etc., compare “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.93.}}

2962:
** κύριος** [ῡ], α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 732, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 143, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306b20}}:—Thess. **κῦρρος** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).517.20 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC): (κῦρος) (not in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}:
**I** of persons, **having power** or **authority over**, with _genitive_, Ζεὺς ὁ πάντων κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).53, compare “P.” 2.58; ἐμῶν τε καὶ σῶν κ. πιστωμάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 878}}; πρὶν ἄν σε κ. στήσω τέκνων put thee **in possession of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1041; κύριοι πολιτείας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.1.1; κ. καταλύσεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20; εἰρήνης καὶ πολέμου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.18; - ώτατοι τοῦ ἱεροῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.53 (but ὁ -ώτατος θεὸς τοῦ ἱεροῦ}}, of the god to whom a temple is dedicated, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.39 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC))}}; τῶν αὑτοὺ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.34, etc.}}; θανάτου κ. τινός **with power of** life and death over, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120d; κ. περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286a24.}}
**I.2** κύριός εἰμι with _infinitive_, I **have authority** to do, **am entitled** to do, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 104 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὗτος κ. ὁρκωμοτεῖν (probably to be read instead of -ῶν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1189; κ. ἀπολέσαι, σῶσαι δ᾽ ἄκυροι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.9, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.63, 8.5}}; -ώτεροι δοῦναι **better able** to give, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.18; οὐ.. κ. οὔτε ἀνελέσθαι πόλεμον οὔτε καταλῦσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.27; δοῦλοι κ. μαστιγοῦν τοὺς ἐλευθέρους 4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus 29 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; αἱ ἀρχαὶ κ. κρίνειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b16}}; also κ. τοῦ μὴ μεθυσθῆναι **having power** not to.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1113b32}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, κ. εἶναι ἢ τοίαν εἶναι [πόλιν] ἢ τοίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 429b.}}
**I.3** followed by a dependent clause, κ. γενέσθαι, ὅντινα δεῖ καταστήσασθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.4.}}
**I.4** with _participle_, πριαμένους τι ἢ πωλοῦντας κυρίους εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.34; κ. ἦν πράσσων ταῦτα [prev. author] 8.51, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.37.8, 18.37.10; κύριοι ἐόντω συλέοντες “Schwyzer” 337.13 (Delph.).}}
**I.5** _absolutely_, **having authority, supreme**, τί τῶνδε κυριωτέρους μένει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 965; - ώτερος σέθεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 505}}; ὁ πατὴρ μέχρι τούτου κ. [ἐστι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1402a1}}; τὸ κ. the **sovereign power** in a state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1281a11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 565a, etc.}}; τὰ κ. the **supreme authorities**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.259, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1365b27; τὰ τῆσδε τῆς γῆς κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 915}}; at Athens, κ. ἐκκλησία a **sovereign** or **principal** assembly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 19, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 43.4, “IG” 12.42.22, +others, 22.493.8, etc.; ἀγορὰ κ. [prev. work]1298.7.}}
**II** of things, ὁ τῆς ὥρας τῆς καταρχῆς κ. [ἀστήρ] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Serapio Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.99}}: but usually _absolutely_, **authoritative, decisive**, δίκαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heracl.” [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 50b}}; μῦθος -ώτερος **of more authority**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 318 (troch.)}}; -ωτάτη τῶν ἐπιστημῶν [ἡ πολιτική] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282b15; αἱ -ώτεραι ἀρχαί [prev. author] “Cael.” 285a26, compare “Metaphorical” 997a12; [ἡ φρόνησις] τῆς σοφίας κυριωτέρα [prev. author] “EN” 1143b34}}; -ωτέρα ἡ καθόλου [ἀπόδειξις] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “APo.” 86a23; τάραχος ὁ -ώτατος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.30U.}}; of **sovereign** remedies, - ωτάτη τῶν καθάρσεων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 230d; - ωτάτη κένωσις 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.299}}; **important, principal**, κ. δόξαι, of certain doctrines of Epicurus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.86 W.; τὰ -ώτατα μέρη τῆς φύσεως 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 9; - ώτερα μέρη τοῦ σώματος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 50}}; τὰ -ώτατα the **principal** organs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.385}} (but, the **most important matters**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 16); τὸ -ώτατον τῆς Ἐφέσου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.22.4: Grammars, κ. τόνος}} **principal** accent, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus “Supp.” 674.32.}}
**II.2** opposed to ἄκυρος, **valid**, νόμος, δόγματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 926d}}; κ. ποιεῖν [τὴν γνῶσιν], opposed to ἄκυρον π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.92, compare 39.15; τὰς συνθήκας κυρίας ποιεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.15; ἡ συγγραφὴ ἥδε κ. ἔστω “PEleph.” 1.14 (4th c.BC)}}; ἔστω τὰ κριθέντα κ. Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.94; so τὰς τῶν ἄλλων δόξας κ. ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179b.}}
**II.3** of times, etc., **ordained, appointed**, ἡ κυρίη ἡμέρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.50, compare 93 (_plural_); ἡ κ. τῶν ἡμερέων [prev. author] 1.48, 6.129; κ. ἐν ἡμέρᾳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 732; τόδε κ. ἦμαρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 105 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; κ. μήν, of a woman with child, i.e. the ninth month, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.32}}; ὅταν τὸ κ. μόλῃ φάος (probably) the **appointed** time, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 766 (Lyric poetry); κ. μένει τέλος [prev. author] “Eu.” 544 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἡ κ. [ἡμέρα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.84, cf. Test.[prev. work] 93}} but αἱ κ. [ἡμεραι], ={κριτικαί}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ παθῶν” 9.}}
**II.4** **legitimate, lawful**, ὕπνος πόνος τε, κ. ξυνωμόται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 127, compare 327}}; κύρι᾽ ἔχοντες having **lawful power**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 960 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.5** ἡ κ. ἀρετή goodness **proper, real** goodness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1144b4; [φλοιὸς] ὁ κ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.15.1}}; Rhet. and Grammars, κ. ὄνομα the **real** or **actual**, hence **current, ordinary**, name of a thing, opposed to μεταφορά, γλῶττα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404b6, 1410b12, “Po.” 1457b3, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 21, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 10.13, etc.}}; σπάνει κυρίου ὀνόματος for lack of a **current** term, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 24}}; -ώτατα ὀνόματα **most ordinary** terms, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3 (hence also κ. ὄνομα}}**proper, personal** name, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.46.10, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 10.11, +others, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.5.8}}; ὄνομα alone in this sense, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Diogenes Babylonius Stoicus 3.213)}}; κ. [λέξεις] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.181 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; κατὰ τὸν κ. τρόπον, opposed to καταχρωμένη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]1.59 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III** _adverb_ κυρίως, see entry
**B** _substantive_ **κύριος**, ὁ, **lord, master**, τοῖσι κ. δωμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 658, compare 689, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 734, etc.}}; ὁ κ. alone, **head** of a family, **master** of a house (compare {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 965), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 6, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1269b10; τοὺς κ. τῶν οἰκιῶν “PTeb.” 5.147 (2nd c.BC)}}; also, **guardian** of a woman, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.32, “PGrenf.” 2.15 i 13 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: generally, **guardian, trustee**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.15, 46.19, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 89, etc.}}
**B.b** later κύριε, as a form of respectful address, **sir,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.12.21, 20.15, NT.Act.16.30 (_plural_), “PFay.” 106.15 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**B.2** _feminine_ κυρία, ἡ, **mistress, lady of the house**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 223, LXX.Isa.24.2, etc.; κ. τῆς οἰκίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 403}}: in _vocative_, **madam**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.44}}; applied to women from fourteen years upwards, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 40}}. (In later Gr. frequently written **κύρα**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 1.61.4 (6th c.AD), etc.)}}
**B.2.b** epithet of Ἶσις, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 180 (Egypt, 1st c.BC), etc.}}
**B.3** of gods, especially in the East, Σεκνεβτῦνις ὁ κ. θεός {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PTeb.” 284.6 (1st c.BC)}}; Κρόνος κ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4521 (Abila, 1st c.AD)}}; Ζεὺς κ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 2.830 (Damascus, 3rd c.AD)}}; κ. Σάραπις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 110.2 (2nd c.AD); ἡ κ. Ἄρτεμις “IG” 4.1124 (Tibur, 2nd c.AD)}}; of deified rulers, τοῦ κ. βασιλέος θεοῦ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 86.8 (Egypt, 1st c.BC)}}; οἱ κ. θεοὶ μέγιστοι, of Ptolemy XIV and {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Cleopatra, “Berl.Sitzb.” 1902.1096: hence, of rulers in general, βασιλεὺς Ἡρώδης κ. “OGI” 415 (Judaea, 1st c.BC)}}; of Roman Emperors, {{Refs|“BGU” 1200.11 (from Augustus), “POxy.” 37 i 6 (from Claudius), etc.}}
**B.4** ὁ Κύριος, = Hebrew {{LXX|“Yahweh”, LXX.Gen.11.5, +others}}; of Christ, {{NT|NT.1Cor.12.3, etc.}}

2963:
** κῡρι-ότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**dominion,** {{NT|NT.Eph.1.21: in _plural_, NT.Col.1.16.}}
**2** later, concrete, **authority,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 151.199 (6th c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **proper, legitimate use of a term**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 306}}; = Latin _proprietas_, {{Refs|Dosith. p.376 K.}}

2964:
** κῡρόω**,
_future_ -ώσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86.β}}: (κῦρος):— **confirm, ratify**, δόμοις.. τήνδ᾽ ἐκύρωσας φάτιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 227 (troch.); τῇδ᾽ ἐκύρωσεν φάτις [prev. work] 521}}; ταῦτα Hdt.[same place]; τὸν γάμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.126; ἡ ἐκκλησία κυρώσασα ταῦτα διελύθη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.69; Ζεῦ, ταῦτα κυρώσειας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 369 (Lyric poetry); μοῖραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 620e; τὴν γνώμην 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.11.1; τὰς διαλύσεις [prev. author] 1.17.1}}:—_middle_, **accomplish one's end**, λόγῳ κυροῦται τὰ πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 451c}}, compare d:—_passive_, **to be ratified, determined**, ἐκεκύρωτο ὁ γάμος Κλεισθένεϊ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.130; οὐδὲ κυρωθῆναι ἔμενον τὸ πρῆγμα [prev. author] 8.56, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.125}}; τοὺς κυρωθέντας [τῶν νόμων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.85, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.93; τὸ ψήφισμα τὸ κυρωθὲν περὶ τούτων “IG” 7.303.45 (Orop.); κυρωθέντος τοῦ δόγματος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.11.3}}; of a contract, **to be sanctioned,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.44 (3rd c.BC)}}; in auctions, **to be knocked down**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“BGU” 992i9 (2nd c.BC)}}; ὁ κυρωθείς the highest bidder, to whom an object is knocked down, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 48.17 (3rd c.BC): generally, ποῖ κεκύρωται τέλος}}; at what point **hath** the end **been fixed** or **determined**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 603, compare “Ch.” 874, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 746 (variant)}}; πρὶν κεκυρῶσθαι σφαγάς before it **has been accomplished**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1069: with}}_infinitive_, ἐκεκύρωτο συμβάλλειν **it had been decided** to fight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.110; ἐκυρώθη ναυμαχέειν [prev. author] 8.56.}}
**2** κ. δίκην **decide** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 581, 639.}}
**3** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **decree** or **ordain** that.., τηρηθῆναι τὸν νόμον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 593.}}
**4** of arguments or doctrines, **confirm, establish**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po. Herc.” 1676.3}}; κ. ὅτι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sign.” 7.}}

2965:
** κύων** [ῠ], ὁ
and ἡ, both in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, the _masculine_ more frequently, _genitive_ κῠνός, _dative_ κῠνί, _accusative_ κύνα}}, _vocative_ κύον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.423, κύων 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 6: _plural_, _nominative_ κύνες, _genitive_ κυνῶν, _dative_ κυσί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.272, +others}}, _Epic dialect_ κύνεσσι {{Refs|1.4, _accusative_ κύνας}}:—**dog, bitch**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; of **shepherds' dogs**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.183, 12.303}}; **watch-dogs**, {{Refs|22.66}}; but in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} more frequently of **hounds**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.338, +others; κυσὶ θηρευτῇσι 11.325; κύνε εἰδότε θήρης 10.360}}; later, when of **hounds**, mostly in _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 8, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 18, etc.; κ. Λάκαινα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 106, S.[same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.1, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 608a27, +others}}; Μολοττικαὶ κ. Alexis Hist. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.54o d, etc.; but Ἰνδικοί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 746a34, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.192: proverbial, κυσὶν πεινῶσιν οὐχὶ βρώσιμα}} 'not fit for a dog', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 1205.4; χεῖρον ἐρεθίσαι γραῦν ἢ κύνα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 802}}; κύνα δέρειν δεδαρμένην 'flog a dead horse', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 179}}; ἡ κ. κατακειμένη ἐν τῇ φάτνῃ 'dog in the manger', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 30, +others}}; χαλεπὸν χορίω κύνα γεῦσαι it's ill to let a dog 'taste blood', {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.11}}; νή or μὰ τὸν κύνα was a favourite oath of Socrates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22a (cf. Scholia), “Grg.” 482b}}; used familiarly at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 83}}; οἷς ἦν μέγιστος ὅρκος.. κύων, ἔπειτα χήν· θεοὺς δ᾽ ἐσίγων, of primitive men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 231.}}
**II** as a word of reproach, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} of women, to denote shamelessness or audacity; applied by Helen to herself, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.344, 356; by Iris to Athena, 8.423; by Hera to Artemis, 21.481}}: of the maids in the house of Odysseus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.338, +others}}: later, in a coarse sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1402}}; ἡ ῥαψῳδὸς κ., of the Sphinx, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 391, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 236 (Lyric poetry)}}; of men, κακαὶ κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.623; implying recklessness, 8.299, 527, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.248, 22.35}}; also of offensive persons, compared to yapping dogs, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.21(22).17, NT.Phil.3.2}}; κ. λαίθαργος, ={λαθροδήκτης}, _metaphorically_, of a person, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 885, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 555: proverbial, μὴ δῶτε τὸ ἅγιον τοῖς κ. NT.Matt.7.6.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, of persons, **watch-dog, guardian**, τῶν σταθμῶν κ., of Agamemnon, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 896}}; δωμάτων κ., of Clytemnestra,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 607, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1023.}}
**II.3** of the Cynics, ἀρέσκει τούτοις κυνῶν μεταμφιέννυσθαι βίον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Sto.Herc.” 339.8}}: hence, **Cynic philosopher**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1411a24, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.65 (Antip.), 413 ([prev. author]), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.717c, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.216b, epigram cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.19, 60}}, Baillet **Inscriptions des tombeaux des rois** {{Refs|172.}}
**III** frequently in Mythology of the **servants, agents** or **watchers** of the gods, Διὸς πτηνὸς κύων, of the eagle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1022, compare “Ag.” 136 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 884}}; of the griffins, Ζηνὸς ἀκραγεῖς κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 803}}; of the Furies, μετάδρομοι.. πανουργημάτων ἄφυκτοι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1388 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 924, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 383}}; Pan is the κύων of Cybele, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 96: 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus, Περσεφόνης κύνες}}, of the planets, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 196: so Comedy texts, Ἡφαίστου κ.}}, of sparks, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 149.16}}; of various mythical beings, as Cerberus, κ. Ἀΐδαο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.368, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.623, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.2.2; Harpies, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.289}}; of Hecate, in Mithraic worship, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.16}}; of the Βάκχαι, Λύσσας κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 977 (Lyric poetry)}}; Λέρνας κ., of the hydra, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 420 (Lyric poetry)}}; of a great fish, Τρίτωνος κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 34.}}
**IV** **dog-fish** or **shark**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.96, compare 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 68, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 161, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 566a31}}; κ. ἄγριος, κ. γαλεός and κ. κεντρίτης or κεντρίνη, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.373, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 1.55}}; ξιφίας κ., of the **sword-fish**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 2.3.}}
**V** = {σείριος} (which see), **dog-star**, i.e. **the hound of Orion**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.29; in full, σειρίου κυνὸς δίκην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 803, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 967; κυνὸς ψυχρὰν δύσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 432.11; πρὸ τοῦ κυνός 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 147}}; μετὰ κυνὸς ἐπιτολήν, περὶ κ. ἐ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 361b35, “HA” 602a26; ἐπὶ κυνί [prev. work] 600a4, Syngr. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.13; ὑπὸ κύνα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 547a14, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.109; περὶ κύνα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.3.3; μετὰ κύνα [prev. author] “HP” 1.9.5}}; also of the whole constellation, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 327, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).17.}}
**VI** **the ace, the worst throw** at dice, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.100, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1289.63.}}
**VII** **frenum praeputii**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 50.3.1}}: with pun on the proverbial cited in Pherecr. [prev. cited] (above {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 158}}: with pun on _signification_see, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.104 (Marc. argument).}}
**VIII** **fetlock of a horse,** {{Refs|Hippiatr. 77.}}
**IX** **unilateral facial paralysis**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.573.}}
**X** ={ἀπομαγδαλία}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1857.19.}}
**XI** ξυλίνη κ., ={κυνόσβατος}, Oracle texts cited in Did cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.70c.}}
**XII** **piece in the game of** πόλεις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 56.3 (uncertain)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _śvā_, _genitive_ **śúnas**, Lithuanian _šuõ_, _genitive_ **šuñs**, Latin _canis_, Gothic _hunds_ (κύων), etc.)

2966:
** κῶλον**, τό,
**limb, member** of a body, especially **leg**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 325, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 183 (Lyric poetry), “Ph.” 42, etc.; δρομάδι κ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1301 (Lyric poetry); κ. ταχύπουν [prev. author] “Ba.” 168 (Lyric poetry)}}: mostly in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 81, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 19; χεῖρες καὶ κῶλα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1185}}: generally, of **arms** and **legs**, and of animals, **fore** and **hind legs**, τὰ ἐμπρόσθια κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91e; τὰ ἔμπροσθεν καὶ τὰ ὄπισθεν κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 498a3, compare “PA” 690a20, etc.; δέρμα, τρίχας, ὄνυχάς τε ἐπ᾽ ἄκροις τοῖς κώλοις ἔφυσαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 76e.}}
**2** ={κωλῆ}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 496.}}
**3** of plants, **limb, arm**, σκολιῆς ἄγρια κ. βάτου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.315 (4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus or 3rd c.BC: Rhianus Epicus)}}: in _plural_, also, **internodes** of the νάρθηξ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 30.}}
**II** generally, **member**,
**II.1** of a building, **side** or **front**, of a square or triangular building, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 134, 4.62, 108, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 947e.}}
**II.1.b** **upright** of a ladder, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 182.5, +others}}
**II.2** **limb** or **lap of the race-course**, διαύλου θάτερον κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” 344.}}
**II.3** Rhet., **member** or **clause of a** περίοδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1409b13, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.165 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22, 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus 9.4.22, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 1, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.3, 2.3}}; στίξομεν κατὰ κῶλον Castor in {{Refs|Rhetores Graeci 3.721}} W.; διελὼν πρὸς κῶλον, of Origen in his Hexapla, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 6.16.}}
**II.4** in verse, **metrical unit containing fewer than three** συζυγίαι **without catalexis**, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus “Poëm.” 1}} **element of a** στροφή, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 19, etc.}}
**II.5** ῥινοῦ ἐΰστροφα κ., _poetry_ for **a sling,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 7.172 (Antip. Sid.).}}
**II.6** incorrect form for κόλον (which see), {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th-7th c.AD: Isidorus Hispalensis Grammaticus “Etym.” 4.7.38, etc.}}; compare κωλικός.

2967:
** κωλύω**,
_future_ -ύσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1448}}: _aorist_ ἐκώλῡσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 897 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 244c}}; _perfect_ κεκώλῡκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.101, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.63 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}:—_passive_, _future_ κωλυθήσομαι {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Sir.20.2(1), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.25}}: also in _middle_ form -ύσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142}}: _aorist_ ἐκωλύθην {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.64, etc.:}}_perfect_ κεκώλῡμαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 37}}. [ῡ always before a _consonant_: common before a vowel, κωλῡόμεσθα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 391, κωλῡέτω [prev. author] “Ph.” 990, κωλῠεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.33, κώλῠει 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55 (= 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 22 Lobel), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.” (see.below), “fragment” 100, 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 25, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 10}}.]:—**hinder, prevent**:—Constr.:
**1** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, κ. ἐκρέειν τὸν Νεῖλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.20}}; κώλυεν [μιν] μεῖναι Pi.[same place]; τί δῆτα καὶ σὲ κωλύει <λαβεῖν> κέρδο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 794, compare “IT” 507, etc.; ὅς σε κ. τὸ δρᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1241; φεύγειν οὐδεὶς κ. νόμος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.52: with _negative_ added (rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose), κ. τινὰ μὴ θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1268; μὴ προσεύχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.22, etc.}}:— _passive_, χρημάτων σπάνει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142}}; τοῦ ὕδατος πιεῖν **from** drinking the water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621b; κωλυόμεσθα μὴ μαθεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 391; μὴ οὐ πονηρὸν εἶναι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.3.}}
**1.b** rarely with _participle_ pro _infinitive_, κ. τινὰ πόλεμον εἰσάγοντα {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}:—_passive_, μὴ κωλύωνται περαιούμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26.}}
**1.c** with _relative_ clause, κωλύειν εἴ τις ἐπαγγέλλεται, = {τινὰ μὴ ἐπαγγέλλεσθαι}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.15}}; ἐκωλύσαμεν, ἵνα γένησθε.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.6.2.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_, κ. τινά τινος **hinder** one **from** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.21, “An.” 1.6.2, etc.; κ. τινὰ ἀπό τινος [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.3.11, 3.3.51}}:—_passive_, τῆς ὁρμῆς ἐκωλύθησαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.55.3.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **prevent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1390 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.24}}:—_passive_, ἐν τούτῳ κεκωλῦσθαι ἐδόκει τὰ πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8, compare 4.14; ταῦτα.. μὴ ἐν ὑμῖν κωλυθῇ [prev. author] 2.64}}; μηδὲ.. δαπάνῃ κεκωλύσθω **let there be** no **hindrance** by reason of expense, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.129.}}
**3.b** **withhold**, τι ἀπό τινος {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.23.6, NT.Luke.6.29.}}
**4** with _accusative_ _person_, **hinder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.35; τοὺς δρῶντας μοχθηρά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1113b26.}}
**5** _absolutely_, οὐδ᾽ ὁ κωλύσων παρῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 261, compare “El.” 1197; εἴσ᾽ οἳ κωλύουσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 499}}; of the tribune's **intercessio**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 10}}; τὸ κωλῦον **hindrance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.12: frequently an}}_infinitive_ may be supplied, εἶτα τίς σε κωλύει (i.e. γεωργεῖν); {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 100}}; αὐτοὶ ὠφελούμενοι τοὺς πολεμίους κωλύσετε [ὠφελεῖσθαι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91, compare 2.37 (_passive_).}}
**6** frequently in {{Refs|3 _person_, οὐδὲν κωλύει}} **there is** nothing **to hinder**, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ὁμόψηφον τὸν Ἀργεῖον εἶναι κ. οὐδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.149; οὐδέν σε κωλύσει σεαυτὸν ἐμβαλεῖν ἐς τὸ βάραθρον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1448, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268e; ὃν διαμάττειν οὐ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 463}}; τί κ. ἡμᾶς διελθεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143a, etc.}}; οὐδὲν κ., _absolutely_, as a form of assent, **be it so**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 723, 972, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272d, etc.}}; τί γὰρ κ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthphr.” 9d, compare “Plt.” 292a, +others; τό γ᾽ ἐμὸν οὐδὲν κ. [prev. author] “Grg.” 458d; μὴ τὸ σὸν κωλυέτω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 990; οὐ τἀμὰ κωλύσει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.151c, etc.}}; οὔτε ἐκεῖνο κωλύει ἐν ταῖς σπονδαῖς neither **is** that **any hindrance**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.144 (wrongly explained as ={κωλύεται} by 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 2.7)}}]; οὔτε μίαν δυοῖν τὴν αὐτὴν εἶναι κ. nor **is there any hindrance** to one of two being the same, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 202b9.}}

2968:
** κώμη**, ἡ,
**unwalled village**, opposed to fortified city (said to be _Doric dialect_ = _Attic dialect_ δῆμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1448a36, compare κωμῳδία), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 18, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.98}}; opposed to πόλις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 626c; κατοικῆσθαι κατὰ κώμας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96}}; πόλεσιν ἀτειχίστοις καὶ κατὰ κ. οἰκουμέναις formed of **scattered villages**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5; πόλεως.. κατὰ κ. τῷ παλαιῷ τῆς Ἑλλάδος τρόπῳ οἰκισθείσης [prev. work] 10, compare 3.94; διοικίζεσθαι κατὰ κώμας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.5; κατὰ κ. κεχωρισμένοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1261a28.}}
**II** **quarter, ward** of a city, διελόμενοι τὴν μὲν πόλιν κατὰ κώμας, τὴν δὲ χώραν κατὰ δήμους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.46, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 746d.}}

2970:
** κῶμος**, ὁ,
**revel, carousal, merry-making**, εἰς δαῖτα θάλειαν καὶ χορὸν ἱμερόεντα καὶ ἐς φιλοκυδέα κ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 481, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 829, 940; πίνειν καὶ κώμῳ χρᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.21, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 804, etc.; κῶμοι καὶ εὐφροσύναι 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.12; δεῖπνα καὶ σὺν αὐλητρίσι κῶμοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173d; ἑορταὶ καὶ κ. [prev. author] “R.” 573d}}; ἐν κώμῳ εἶναι, of a city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.25; ἔρχεσθαί τισιν ἐπὶ κῶμον [prev. author] “Smp.” 2.1; ἐπὶ κῶμον βαδίζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1040}}; especially in honour of gods, τοῖς ἐν ἄστει Διονυσίοις ἡ πομπὴ.. καὶ ὁ κ. Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.10, compare “IG” 2.971, etc.}}; κώμῳ θυραμάχοις τε πυγμαχίαισι Pratin.Lyric poetry 1.8; χοροῖς ἢ κώμοις Ὑακίνθου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1469 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** concrete, **band of revellers**, κ. εὐίου θεοῦ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 1167 (Lyric poetry)}}; especially of the **procession** which celebrated a victor in games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.22, etc.}}: generally, **rout, band**, κ. Ἐρινύων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1189}}; of an army, κ. ἀναυλότατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 791 (Lyric poetry); κ. ἀσπιδηφόρος [prev. author] “Supp.” 390}}; **band** of hunters, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 55}}; of maidens, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1184}}; of doves, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 1197.}}
**II** **the ode sung** at one of these festive processions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.20, 70, “O.” 4.10, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8.103; μελιγαρύων τέκτονες κώμων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 104, 988 (both Lyric poetry).}}

2974:
** κωφ-ός**, ή, όν,
**blunt, dull, obtuse**, opposed to ὀξύς, κ. βέλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.390, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 495.27; κ. καλάμη “Anthologia Graeca” 12.25 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Statyllius Flaccus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** _metaphorically_,
**II.1** of sound, **mute, noiseless**, κύματι κωφῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.16}}; κωφὴν γὰρ δὴ γαῖαν ἀεικίζει is maltreating **dumb, senseless** earth, {{Refs|24.54}}; τὰ μὲν ἄλλα ἔσκε κωφά the other parts sounded **dull**, opposed to to the ringing of the hollow parts when struck, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.200}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, κωφὰ δὲ πόντος κεῖτο {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1103}}; ὁ κ. λιμήν, probably the bay of Munychia, as opposed to to the noisy Piraeus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.31}}; κωφότερος ὁ ψόφος ἔσται, i.e. **muffled**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 19}}; τῶν μεταλλικῶν κωφότατος [ὁ σίδηρος] **rings least**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.721f; κωφοὶ ἄνεμοι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.51.}}
**II.2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, of men or animals, **dumb**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 6.7, etc.}}; καὶ κωφοῦ συνίημι καὶ οὐ φωνεῦντος ἀκούω Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.47}}; οὐ.. παρὰ κωφὸν ὁ τυφλὸς ἔοικε λαλῆσαι, i.e. is not so **dumb** but that he will answer the blind fool who assails him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 6; κωφότερος κίχλης 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 29}}; κ. χάρις a **mute** gift (i.e. an epitaph), {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 298 (from Teos)}}; so κωφοῖς δάκρυσι {{Refs|“IPE” 2.299 (from Panticapaeum)}}; κ. τάφοι probably in {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).441.26}}; κ. προσωπεῖον **mute** figure on the stage, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.520, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.791e; κ. πρόσωπον 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.19.3}}; κ. καὶ ἄλογος, of a house, **with no echoes**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 1.}}
**II.2.b** **deaf,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Mercurium 92, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 34. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 202, “Ch.” 881; λήθην κωφήν, ἄναυδον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 670}}; ὅσοι γίνονται κ. ἐκ γενετῆς, πάντες καὶ ἐνεοὶ γίνονται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 536b3}} (hence of a **deaf and dumb** person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, “BGU” 1196.49 (1st c.BC),compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus); with _genitive_, κωφὴ ἀκοῆς αἴσθησις 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 196.5, conjecture in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 932a}}; κ. Ἑλλάδος φωνᾶς **deaf** of one's Greek ear, i.e. ignorant of Greek, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Dialexeis 5th c.BC” 6.12; σπαράγματα κωφὰ τοῦ βεβαιοῦντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1108d.}}
**II.2.c** _metaphorically_, νοῦς ὁρῇ καὶ νοῦς ἀκούει· τἆλλα κωφὰ καὶ τυφλά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 249; κ. πέτρος 4th c.BC: Moschio Tragicus Trag. 7; μαψαῦραι 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 67; ἐρημία 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.40}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, κωφὰ χλιαίνεσθαι **feebly,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 12.125 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3** ὄμμα κ. **vacant, lack-lustre** eye, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 807b23.}}
**II.4** of the senses in general, **dull**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 19 (_comparative_).}}
**II.5** of the mind, **dull, obtuse**, ἐγὼ ὁ πάντα κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 911, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.87; τὸ τῆς ψυχῆς ποιεῖν κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 88b: κωφοί, οἱ, 'the Dullards', title of satyr-play by Sophocles.}}
**II.5.b** of things, **senseless, unmeaning, obscure**, κ. καὶ παλαί᾽ ἔπη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 290; κ. διήγησις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.36.4, compare 5.21.4; ὑπόνοια 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.71 K.; σκῶμμα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.712a}}; but κ. εὐπραγίαι is probably _falsa lectio_ for{κοῦφαι}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.27.}} _adverb_ -φῶς **obscurely**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 251.25}}: _comparative_ -ότερον, ἐνοχλεῖν **less acutely**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.21 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}

2975:
** λαγχάνω**,
_future_ λήξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 617e}}; _Ionic dialect_ λάξομαι (compare λάξις) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144}}: _aorist_ ἔλᾰχον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.367, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔλλαχον [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 87, variant for{ἔλλαβεν} in 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.271}}]; _Epic dialect_ λάχον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.49, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _optative_ _1st pers._ _singular_ λαχόην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 9 (λαχοίην 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 247.25)}}; for λέλᾰχον see infr. IV: _perfect_ εἴληχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 376, 423, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ εἰλήχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d}}; _poetry_ and _Ionic dialect_ λέλογχα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.53, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.39, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 20.3, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 282 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.53, Test. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.82, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.83, etc.}}, but not in early _Attic dialect_ Prose; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ λελογχᾰσι (ν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.304, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 102, but λελᾰχᾱσι [prev. author] 115.5}}; _participle_ λελαχώς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.17}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ λελόγχει {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.40}}: _pluperfect_ ἐλελόγχει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 18}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐλήχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.8, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.24, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.20}}: _perfect_ εἴληγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 296, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.34}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ λελάχαται {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Perictione Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.28.19:}}
**I** with _accusative_ _of things_, **obtain by lot**, of spoils, opposed to ἐξαιρεῖσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.233, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.367, etc.}}: generally, **obtain as one's portion**, τὸ γὰρ λάχομεν γέρας ἡμεῖς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.49; λαχόντα τε ληΐδος αἶσαν 18.327; πρὸς δαιμόνων ὄλβον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.45; μέζονας μοίρας λ. 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 25; μοῖραν ἴσην, ὡς αὐτοί περ ἐλάγχανον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.282, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144: with}}_infinitive_ added, ἔλαχον πολιὴν ἅλα ναιέμεν I **had** the sea **for my portion** to dwell in, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.190, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.34, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 931 (_anapaest meter_); ἔλαχ᾽ ἄναξ δούλην σ᾽ ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 278, compare 282 (Lyric poetry)}}; of a deity as presiding over one's life, ἐμὲ μὲν Κὴρ.. λάχε γεινόμενόν περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.79; τῶ σκληρῶ μάλα δαίμονος ὅς με λελόγχει 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.40; δαίμων ὁ τὴν ἡμετέραν μοῖραν εἰληχώς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.78; ὦ δαῖμον, ὅς με.. εἴληχας 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.49}}: also, especially in _perfect_, **to be the tutelary deity of** a place, **protect** it. [Πὰν] πάντα λόφον.. λέλογχε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 19.6; θεοῖσι οἳ Περσίδα γῆν λελόγχασι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.53; παῖ Ῥέας, ἃ πρυτανεῖα λέλογχας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.1}}; of Athena, ἣ τὴν ὑμετέραν πόλιν ἔλαχε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 23d, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 319 (Lyric poetry), “Ph.” 1576 (Lyric poetry): _metaphorically_, ἀκέρδεια λέλογχεν θαμινὰ κακαγόρος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.53}}: frequently of persons **who have a post assigned to them by lot**, κλήρῳ νῦν πεπάλασθε διαμπερές, ὅς κε λάχῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.171, compare 179, 23.354, 862}}: with _infinitive_, κλήρῳ λάχον ἐνθάδ᾽ ἕπεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|24.400; so πάλῳ λαχεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 55, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.94, compare 3.128; ὡς ἐν πύλαις ἕκαστος εἴληχεν πάλον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 376: _absolutely_, πρὸς Θύμβρης ἔλαχον Δύκιοι}} **had their post assigned** near Thymbra, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.430}}; ἐπί, ἐν πύλαις λ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 423, 451, etc.}}; λαχών alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128, etc.}}; λ. τινὰ διδάσκαλον **have him assigned to one by lot**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.11.}}
**I.2** at Athens, **obtain** an office **by lot**, ἀρχὰς λαχεῖν, opposed to χειροτονηθῆναι (to be elected), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1111; οὐδεμίαν [ἀρχὴν] λαχὼν οὐδὲ χειροτονηθείς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.106: more frequently with}}_infinitive_, ὁ τῷ κυάμῳ λαχὼν πολεμαρχέειν **he who had the lot** to be polemarch, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109; ἔλαχον πρότεροι ἀποδιδόναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.21, compare 35; λαχὼν.. ἱερομνημονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 623}}; λαχόντος βουλεύειν **when I became** Member of Council **by lot**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.111, compare 59.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473e: with _genitive_, λαχεῖν τῶν ἐξιόντων}} **to be chosen by lot** as one of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.133; also οἱ ταμίαι οἱ λαχόντες “IG” 12.91.21}}; λαχεῖν βασιλεύς, ἐπιμελητής, ἱερεύς, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.47, etc.; ἐπίσκοπος τῷ κυάμῳ λαχών 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1022}}; οἱ πεντακόσιοι [οἱ] λαχόντες τῷ κυάμῳ Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.96: _absolutely_, κληρούχους τοὺς λαχόντας}} **those on whom the lot fell**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 765c; τοὺς λαχόντας προέδρους “SIG” 465.6 (Athens, 3rd c.BC); rare except in Athens, λαχὼν ἱερεύς [prev. work]762.12 (Dionysopolis, 1st c.BC), etc.}}
**I.3** as _Attic dialect_ law-term, λαγχάνειν δίκην **obtain leave to bring** a suit (especially a private suit), probably because the presiding magistrates decided the order of hearing by lot; λ. δίκην τινί **against** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 5b, “Lg.” 938b, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.99; ἔγκλημά τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.16}}; τὸν εἰληχότα τοῦ κλήρου τὴν δίκην the person **suing** for the inheritance, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.3: without τὴν δίκην, εἴληχε μὲν αὑτῷ τῆς θυγατρὸς τῆς Εὐκτήμονος ὡς οὔσης ἐπικλήρου}} he **has claimed** Euctemon's daughter.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.46, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.20; λ. τινὶ τοῦ συμβολαίου 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.3; λ. φόνου ἐμαυτῷ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.120}}; also λ. τῷ υἱεῖ τῆς ἐπικλήρου **prosecute the claim** on his son's behalf, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.121, compare 124; λ. δίκην τινὶ εἰς τοὺς Ἀμφικτύονας χιλίων ταλάντων ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.98, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.2: _absolutely_, λ. πρὸς τὸν ἄρχοντα 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.33: _metaphorically_, τοῦ σώματος [τῇ ψυχῇ] δίκην λαχόντος 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 159}}:—_passive_, πρὸς οὓς αἱ δίκαι ἐλήχθησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.8; πρὸ τοῦ τὴν δίκην ληχθῆναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.28: _impersonal_, τούτοις λαγχάνεται}} **proceedings are taken**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.76.}}
**II** with _genitive_ _partitive_, **become possessed of** a thing, ὥς κεν Ἀχιλλεὺς δώρων ἐκ Πριάμοιο λάχῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.76; ἔλαχον κτερέων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.311, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 934, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).69, “fragment” 75.6, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.56, 9.39, “Lyrica Adespota” 53, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 102, 115.5, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 21; εὖ πραπίδων λαχόντα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 380 (Lyric poetry); χρυσῆς.. τιμῆς λαχεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 699; οὔ τι μὴ λάχωσι τοῦδε συμμάχου [prev. author] “OC” 450}}; γέννας ἀφθίτου λαχόντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 278; διπλοῦ βίου λαχόντες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1086}}; πατρῴων οὐ λαχών not **having obtained** thy patrimony, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1192; τῆς εὐπρεπεστάτης τελευτῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44; δείπνου τε καὶ ὕπνου λαγχάνομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 6.9; also χθονὸς λαχεῖν τοσοῦτον ἐνθανεῖν μόνον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 790; γάμου μέρος λαχοῦσα [prev. author] “Ant.” 918; τύμβου κοινὸν εἰληχὼς μέρος [prev. author] “El.” 1135; τῆς γῆς τὸ πρὸς Νότον εἴληχε Παλλάς [prev. author] “fragment” 24.8.}}
**III** _absolutely_, **draw lots**, κατάστασις ἡ διὰ τοῦ λαγχάνειν γιγνομένη {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.23, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.63, etc.; περί τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21 argument 2 3, 4, NT.John.19.24.}}
**IV** causal only in _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ _aorist_ λέλᾰχον, **put in possession of** a thing, **grant** one **the rights of**.., ὄφρα πυρός με Τρῶες.. λελάχωσι θανόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.80, compare 15.350, 23.76}}: later this _aorist_ is used _intransitive_ in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.341 (5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus).}}
**V** _intransitive_, **fall to one's lot** or **share**, ἐς ἑκάστην [νῆα] ἐννέα λάγχανον αἶγες nine goats **were allotted** to each, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.160; αἰὼν δυσαίων ἔλαχεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 214 (Lyric poetry); ὅσοις.. τὸ σωφρονεῖν εἴληχεν [prev. author] “Hipp.” 80; τὸ λαχὸν μέρος ἑκάστῳ τῷ θεῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 745e, compare “Epin.” 992d; τὴν πρὸς Νότον λαχεῖν φασι Δευκαλίωνι 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.5.23.}}

2979:
** λακτίζω**,
_perfect_ λελάκτῐκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 136}}:—**kick with the heel** or **foot**, λ. ποσὶ γαῖαν, of a defeated boxer, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.99, compare 22.88}}; φλὸξ αἰθέρα λακτίζοισα καπνῷ flames **lashing** heaven with smoke, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).66}}; κραδία δὲ φόβῳ φρένα λακτίζει my heart '**knocks at** my ribs' for fear, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 881 (_anapaest meter_); [ἔρως] λ. κραδίην “Anthologia Graeca” 12.16 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)}}; τὸν πεσόντα λακτίσαι **trample on** the fallen, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 885}}; λ. βωμὸν εἰς ἀφάνειαν **trample on** the altar so as utterly to destroy it, {{Refs|[prev. work] 383 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὴν θύραν λ. **kick at** the door, Ar.[same place]; λ. ἀλλήλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 586b}}; of horses, λ. τὸ λυποῦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 690a21; ἑαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516a; ὑπὸ ἵππου λακτισθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.18: _metaphorically_, λ. πολλὴν χάριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 411}}; βοῦς ὁ λακτίσας ὑμᾶς, of a clumsy-footed person, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.118}}:—_middle_ in _active_ sense, {{Refs|“Mim. Oxy.” 413.65.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **kick, struggle**, {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 90}}; of horses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Eq. Mag.” 1.4: frequently in proverbial λ. ποτὶ κέντρον, πρὸς κέντρα}}, **kick** against the pricks, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.95, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1624, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 795, NT.Act.26.14, etc.; so πρὸς κῦμα λ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1396.}}

2980:
** λᾰλ-έω**,
**talk, chat, prattle**, ἕπου καὶ μὴ λάλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1058, compare “V.” 1135; ἡ μὲν χελιδὼν τὸ θέρος.. λαλεῖ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 208; λαλεῖς.. ἀμελήσας ἀποκρίνασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 287d}}: with _dative_, **talk to** one, λαλῶν ἐν ταῖς ὁδοῖς σεαυτῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 348; αὑτοῖς 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 11; πρὸς αὑτούς 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 9.10; λ. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 627; ὑπέρ τινος 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 26.3}}; opposed to λέγω, λαλεῖν ἄριστος, ἀδυνατώτατος λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 95}}; λαλῶν μὲν.., λέγων δέ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.118 (assuming variant); λαλεῖν τι ἡμῖν ὅπως ἂν ἡμᾶς ὕπνος λάβῃ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 7.10: hence}}
**b** generally, **talk, speak**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 110 (variant for{λακεῖν}); καινὴν διάλεκτον λ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 171; Ἀττικιστὶ λ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 195.4. with _metaphorically_, ζωγραφία λαλοῦσα (of poetry), opposed to ποίησις σιωπῶσα (of painting), 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.346f.}}]
**2** **talk of**, τινα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus “fragment” 5.2; ἀλλήλαις λαλέουσι τεὸν γάμον αἱ κυπάρισσοι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.58; ἅμαξαν “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.92}}:—_passive_, πρᾶγμα κατ᾽ ἀγορὰν λαλούμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 578.}}
**3** in later writers, ={λέγω}, **speak**, λαλεῖ οὐθὲν τῶν ἄλλων ζῴων πλὴν ἀνθρώπου {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 899a1: frequently in LXX, LXX.Gen.12.4, +others; βασιλέως ἐναντίον 2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 118; πρός τινα NT.Act.3.22, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 3, etc.; περὶ τῆς λέξεως 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.32, compare “Rh.” 1.189 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others}}; χειρσὶν ἅπαντα λαλήσας, of a pantomime, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“IG” 14.2124: _absolutely_, εἴ τι μὴ λίθος, τοὔργον, ἐρεῖς, λαλήσει 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.33, compare 6.61; ἐλάλησεν ὁ κωφός NT.Matt.9.33:—}}_passive_, λαληθήσεταί σοι ὅ τι σε δεῖ ποιεῖν **it shall be told** thee.., {{NT|NT.Act.9.6.}}
**II** **chatter**, opposed to articulate speech, as of locusts, **chirp**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.34}}; μεσημβρίας λαλεῖν τέττιξ (i.e. εἰμί), a very grasshopper to **chirp** at midday, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 10.6; ἀνθρωπίνως λ. 3rd c.BC(?): Strato Comicus 1.46.}}
**III** of musical sounds, αὐλῷ λαλέω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.29}}; of trees,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|see.above 1.2; δι᾽[αὐλοῦ ἢ σάλπιγγος] λ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 801a29}}; of Echo, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 74.14: also c._accusative_ _cognate_, μάγαδιν λαλεῖν}} **sound** the μάγαδις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 35.}}

2981:
** λᾰλ-ιά**,
_poetry_ **λᾰλ-ιή**, ἡ, **talk, chat**, λαλιὰν ἀσκῆσαι, ἐπιτηδεῦσαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 931 (_anapaest meter_), “Ra.” 1069; πέρας ποιεῖ λαλιᾶς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 66.3, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.78, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.440 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; **common talk, report**, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.20.5; τῆς εὐανδρίας τινός LXX.2Mac.8.7; ἀχέων “APl.” 4.134 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); λαλιάν τινα ποιεῖν LXX.Sir.42.11}}; in good sense, **discussion**, ἡ περὶ βυβλίων λ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.23.4, compare 36.12.3}}; **speech, conversation,** {{NT|NT.John.8.43}}; **matter, subject**, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.3.18.}}
**2** **loquacity**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.49, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 7, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 806b18, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 46.}}
**II** **a form of speech, dialect,** {{LXX+NT|NT.Matt.26.73; ἡ λ. σου ὡραία LXX.Cant.4.3}}; **style**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.27 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}

2983:
** λαμβάνω**,
_future_ λήψομαι (λήψω only late, variant in {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.4.18)}}; _Ionic dialect_ λάψομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“GDI” 5497.3, +others (Milet., 4th-3rd c.BC), 5597.11 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC), corrupted to λάμψομαι in Mss. of 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199}}; _Doric dialectfuture_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ λαψῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34.2, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.4, 10}}, _infinitive_ λαμψεῖσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PSI” 9.1091.19; Hellenistic λήμψομαι “PPar.” 14.47 (2nd c.BC), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4224c (add.) (from Telmessus), 4244 (from Tlos), +others:}}_aorist 2_ ἔλᾰβον, _Epic dialect_ ἔλλᾰβον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.170, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ Iterat. λάβεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 112, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.78, 130; _imperative_ λαβέ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.407, etc.}}; written λάβε in _middle_ Ms. of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 130, but λαβέ}} _Attic dialectaccusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 1.431}}: _perfect_ εἴληφα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 643, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 591 (Lyric poetry), etc. (uncertain in 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 143)}}; _Ionic dialect_, _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus λελάβηκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79, “IG” 42(1).121.68 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5(2).6.14 (Tegea, 4th c.BC), also 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 426}}; _infinitive_ λελαβήκειν {{Refs|“IG” 42(1).121.59 (Epid.), “PSI” 9.1091.7}}: _pluperfect_ εἰλήφειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.88, _Ionic dialect3rd.pers._ _sing._ λελαβήκεε variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42}} (κατα-); _Doric dialect_ _perfect_ _subjunctive_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ (παρ-) λελόνβῃ {{Refs|“GDI” 5087b1 (from Crete):}} —_middle_, _aorist 2_ ἐλαβόμην, _Epic dialect_ ἐλλ-, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.325, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ λελαβέσθαι {{Refs|4.388}}:—_passive_, _future_ ληφθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 68, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91, κατα-λελήψομαι 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 54p.677D.}}: _aorist_ ἐλήφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 101, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἐλάφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 58.8 (Milet., 5th c.BC), (κατ-) “GDI” 5532.7 (from Zeleia), ἐλάμφθην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89, 6.92, 7.239 (-λάφθ- by erasure in codex B)}}; Hellenistic ἐλήμφθην {{NT|“IG” 14.1320, NT.Mark.16.19 (ἀν-)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐλάφθην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “ψαμμίτης” 1.13}}: _perfect_ εἴλημμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 455}}; but in Trag.usually λέλημμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 876, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1113, “IA” 363 (troch.), “Cyc.” 433, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1090}} (δια-); so later προ-λέληπτε (sic) {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 2.769 (from Dura)}}; _Ionic dialect_ λέλαμμαι (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.51, (δια-) 3.117}}; _infinitive_ ἀνα-λελάφθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 11}} (_accusative_ to many codices, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographusand 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus, -λελάμφθαι vulgar form)}}; _Ionic dialect3rd.pers._ _plural_ λελήφαται {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.268}}; _Doric dialect_ _perfect_ _imperative_ λελάφθω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 3, +others}}:—in the _future_, _aorist_ _passive_, and _perfect_ _passive_ the **a** is short by nature in _Ionic dialect_, probably long in _Doric dialect_ and in Doric dialect ized Hellenistic forms such as λαμψοῦνται {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Test.Epict.” 5.14, λάμψεσθαι “IG” 5(1).1390.67 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; it is marked long in _Aeolic dialect_ λᾱμψεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 5.9:—of these tenses 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only}}_aorist_ _active_, and _aorist_ _middle_ twice (see. above); the Homeric _present_ is λάζομαι. —The word has two main senses, one (more active) **take**; the other (more passive) **receive**:
**I** **take**,
**I.1** **take hold of, grasp, seize**, μάστιγα καὶ ἡνία {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.81: frequently with χειρί or χερσί added, χειρὶ χεῖρα λαβόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.286; χερμάδιον λάβε χειρί 5.302; χείρεσσι λαβὼν περιμήκεα κοντόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.487; ἐν χείρεσσι λάβ᾽ ἡνία 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.116; ἐν χεροῖν λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 913; διὰ χερῶν λαβών [prev. author] “Ant.” 916; ἐς χέρας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1242; ἐν ἀγκάλαις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 481, etc.}}; of an eagle, λ. ἄγραν ποσίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.81}}: c._accusative_ of the thing seized, λ. γούνατα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.465}}; but also with _accusative_ of whole, _genitive_ of part seized, τὴν πτέρυγος λάβεν **caught** her **by** the wing,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.316; τὸν δὲ πεσόντα ποδῶν ἔλαβε 4.463; γούνων λαβὼν κούρην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.142; λ. τινὰ τῆς ζώνης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.10, etc.: sometimes with _genitive_ only, ἀγκὰς ἀλλήλων λαβέτην χερσί}} they **took hold of** one another with their arms, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.711}}:—frequently in _middle_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**I.1.b** **take by violence, carry off as prize** or **booty**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.273, 8.191, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.130, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 68 (_passive_), 1431, etc.}}; **capture** a city, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.24.11, 3.61.8; ἐκ πόλιος.. ἀλόχους καὶ κτήματα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.41}}; of lions, λαβὼν κρατεροῖσιν ὀδοῦσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.114; ἵνα δαῖτα λάβῃσιν 24.43}}; of an eagle, {{Refs|17.678}}; of a dolphin, {{Refs|21.24.}}
**I.1.c** λ. δίκην **take, exact** punishment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.29,34, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.181; ποινάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 360, etc. (rarely for δοῦναι δίκην, see.below 11.1 e); λ. τιμωρίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.280.}}
**I.2** of passions, feelings, etc., **seize**, μένος ἔλλαβε θυμόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.468; Ἀτρεΐωνα.. χόλος λάβεν 1.387; ὁππότε κέν μιν γυῖα λάβῃ κάματος 4.230; τὸν δὲ τρόμος ἔλλαβε γυῖα 24.170, +others; δὴν δέ μιν ἀμφασίη ἐπέων λάβε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.704; τοὺς Ἀθηναίους θάρσος ἔλαβε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.92; ἄχος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.6; δέος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 699c}}; ἐπειδὴ καιρὸς ἐλάμβανε when the occasion **came to** them, i.e. **occurred**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 44.19}}; of fevers and sudden illnesses, **attack**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.19, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 417, etc. (compare λάζομαι, λῆψις):}} —_passive_, λαμβάνεσθαι νόσῳ, ὑπὸ [νόσου], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 446, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.138; ἔρωτι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.31, etc. (reversely of the person, λ. θυμόν, etc., see below 11.3).}}
**I.2.b** of a deity, **seize, possess**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79}}:—_passive_, τῇ Ῥέᾳ λαμβάνονται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 37.}}
**I.2.c** of darkness, etc., **occupy, possess**, εὖτ᾽ ἂν κνέφας τεμενος αἰθέρος λάβῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 365.}}
**I.3** **catch, overtake**, as an enemy, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.159, 11.106, 126, etc.; λ. τινὰ στείχοντα θύραζε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.418; ζῶντες ἐλάμφθησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.119}}; simply, **find, come upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1031, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1339.}}
**I.4** **catch, find out, detect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89 (_passive_)}}; ποίῳ λαβών σε Ζεὺς ἐπ᾽ αἰτιάματ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 196; τὸν αὐτόχειρα τοῦ φόνου λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 266: frequently with}}_participle_, κἂν λάβῃς ἐψευσμένον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 461; κλέπτοντα Κλέωνα λάβοιμι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 759; λ. τινὰ ψευδόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 389d; τοῦτον ὑβρίζοντα λαβόντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.97}}: with _adjective_, ὅπως μὴ λήψομαί σε προπετῆ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 570}}:—_passive_, δρῶσ᾽ ἐλήφθης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 808; ἐπ᾽ αὐτοφώρῳ δεινὰ δρῶντ᾽ εἰλημμένω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 455; ληφθεῖσαν ἐπ᾽ αὐτοφώρῳ μηχανωμένην τι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.3; ἐλήφθη μοιχός 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.66}}: in good sense, οὐκ ἂν λάβοις μου μᾶλλον οὐδέν᾽ εὐσεβῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1051.}}
**I.5** λ. τινὰ πίστι καὶ ὁρκίοισι **bind** him by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.74; ἀραῖον λαβεῖν τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 276 codices}}
**I.6** with _double_ _accusative_, **take as**, λαβὼν πρόβλημα σαυτοῦ παῖδα τόνδ᾽ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1007}}; ξυμπαραστάτην λ. τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 675; τοὺς Ἕλληνας λ. συναγωνιζομένους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.86.}}
**I.7** τὴν Ἴδην λαβὼν ἐς ἀριστερὴν χεῖρα **taking, keeping** Ida to your left (nisi to be read λαβών, ἐς..) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.42; ἐν δεξιᾷ λ. τὴν Σικελίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.1}}; λ. τὸ στρατόπεδον κατὰ νώτου **take** in rear, i.e. be behind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75; compare ἀπείργω 11.2, ἔχω (A) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.7.}}
**I.8** λ. Ἑλληνίδα ἐσθῆτα **assume** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.78, compare 2.37; λ. ζυγόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.93.}}
**I.8.b** **take** food or drugs, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Diocles Epigrammaticus “Frr.” 121 (_passive_), 140, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.125, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.469.}}
**I.9** **apprehend by the senses**, ὄμμασιν θέαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 537, compare 656}}; πρόσφθεγμά τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 234; ὁρᾶται, ἢ ἄλλῃ τινὶ αἰσθήσει λαμβάνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 524d.}}
**I.9.b** **apprehend with the mind, understand**, φρενὶ λ. τὸν λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.10; νόῳ [prev. author] 3.41; τῇ διανοίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 143a; λ. ἐν ταῖς γνώμαις βεβαίως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.51; ἐν νῷ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.35.6: _absolutely_, λ. τὴν ἀλήθειαν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.6; μνήμην παρὰ τῆς φήμης λ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246d, etc.}}
**I.9.c** with _adverb_ added, **take**, i.e. **understand** in a certain manner, ταύτῃ ταῦτα ἐλάμβανον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.142; λάβετε [τοὺς λόγους] μὴ πολεμίως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.17}}; τὸ πρᾶγμα μειζόνως ἐλάμβανον **took** it more seriously, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.27, compare 61; ὀρθῶς λ. τὸν φιλοκερδῆ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 227c}}; λ. τι οὕτω, ὧδε, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 174b27, “Rh.Al.” 1423a4; ὀργῇ καὶ φόβῳ τὸ γεγονὸς λ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 18: with παρά with _accusative_, λαμβάνω σε παρὰ βουκόλον.. “PMag.Par.” 1.2434:}} —_passive_, τρίτου καθεστῶσαι ἐπὶ πρώτου λαμβάνονται **are used for** the first person, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 78.22}}; with ἐς, εἰ ἐς κόρην λαμβάνοιτο **be taken** for a girl, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.32: less frequently with double _accusative_, ὡς μεθυστικὰς λ. [τὰς ἁρμονίας] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1342b25, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.179; τῆς νίκης ἆθλον τὴν ὑπεροχὴν τῆς πολιτείας λ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1296a31; τοῦτο λ. γιγνόμενον [prev. author] “Mete.” 346a7; also λ. περί τινος τί ἐστι [prev. author] “EN” 1142a32, compare 1140a24, +others}}: also with _infinitive_, λ. τι εἶναί τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mete.” 389a29, +others: with a _relative_ clause, οὕτω δεῖ λαμβάνειν, ἀλλ᾽ οὐχ ὅτι.. [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1053a27, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.1; εἰλήφθω ὁ ἄδικος ποσαχῶς λέγεται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1129a31}}: in bad sense, πρὸς δέους λ. τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 7; πρὸς ἀτιμίας [prev. author] “Cic.” 13; λ. δι᾽ οἴκτου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 194}}; but also ἐν χάριτι καὶ δωρεᾷ λ. **receive** as a favour, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.31.6.}}
**I.9.d** in Logic, **assume, take for granted**, ἅπαν ζῷον λαμβάνει ἢ θνητὸν ἢ ἀθάνατον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 46b6; λ. τὰς περὶ ἕκαστον ἀρχάς [prev. work] 53a2, etc.:}} —_passive_, τὰ ἐξ ἀρχῆς ληφθέντα{{Refs|[prev. work] 26b30}}; αἱ εἰλημμέναι προτάσεις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 33a15, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.46 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Sign.” 35, “Oec.” p.5 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.89.}}
**I.9.e** **take**, i.e. **determine, estimate**, τὴν ξυμμέτρησιν τῶν κλιμάκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20; ἐντεῦθεν τὸ μέγεθος τῶν ἁμαρτημάτων 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 66; τὴν τιμωρίαν ποθεινοτέραν λ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42.}}
**I.10** **take in hand, undertake** (compare ληπτέον), λ. τι ἐπὶ τὸ σωφρονέστερον, opposed to συνταχύνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71}}; μηδένα πόνον λαβόντες without **taking** any trouble, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.24; παλαισμάτων λ. φροντίδα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.22.}}
**I.11** **take in, hold**, τὸ στρατόπεδον πεζοὺς λ. περὶ τετρακισχιλίους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.107.10.}}
**I.12** _participle_ λαβών frequently seems pleonastic, but adds dramatic effect, λαβὼν κύσε χεῖρα **took and** kissed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.398, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.36: so in Trag. and Comedy texts, τί μ᾽ οὐ λαβὼν ἔκτεινα; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1391, compare 641; τῆ νῦν τόδε πῖθι λαβών 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 141, etc.}}
**I.12.b** ingressive of ἔχων (ἔχω (A) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.6), ἑτάρους τε λ. καὶ νῆα.. ἦλθον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.269, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 259.}}
**II** **receive**,
**II.1** **have given one, get, receive**, properly of things {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 106), ἄποινα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.427; τὰ πρῶτα 23.275; ἀντίποινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 592}}, see belowe; παρὰ βασιλέος δῶρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 439; πρός τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 12, etc.; ἀπὸ τῶν συκοφαντῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.4}}; **gain, win**, κλέος [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.298, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1347, etc.; ἀρετάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.6; κόσμον [prev. author] “N.” 3.31 codices (variant{ἔλαχες} Scholia)}}]; ἀλκήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 218, etc.}}; πρὸς τὸ μνηστεύεσθαι λ. ἡλικίαν **attain**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.39; λ. νόστον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1016, etc.; λ. τὴν ἀρχὴν τῆς θαλάττης 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.61; μοναρχίαν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1163; τέρψιν [prev. author] “Tr.” 820; χάριν [prev. author] “OT” 1004; κέρδος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 906}}: also in bad sense, λ. ὀνείδη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1494; συμφοράν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 43; θάνατον [prev. author] “Hel.” 201 (Lyric poetry)}}; γέλωτα μωρίαν τε **incur**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ion” 600; αἰτίαν ἀπό τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.18, etc.:—for λ. θυμόν, etc., see above 1.2 et below 3.}}
**II.1.b** **receive hospitably**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.255, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 284}} (ἔλαβες τὸν ἱκέτην ἐχέγγυον) which approaches this sense; καλῶς λ. τινά **treat** well, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 843.10 (1st-2nd c.AD).}}
**II.1.c** **receive in marriage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199, 9.108, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 953.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.39, “PEleph.” 1.2 (4th c.BC), 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 436}}; τοῖς λαμβάνουσιν ἐξ αὐτῶν, i.e. those who married their daughters, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1044.14 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; also of the father taking a daughter-in-law, τῷ υἱῷ λ. τινά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 447.}}
**II.1.d** λ. ὄνομα, ἐπωνυμίαν, **receive** a name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 305d, “Smp.” 173d.}}
**II.1.e** λ. δίκην **receive**, i.e. **suffer**, punishment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115}}; τὴν ἀξίην λ. **get** one's deserts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.39; δίκην γὰρ ἀξίαν ἐλάμβανεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1312; λ. ζημίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.11.}}
**II.1.f** λ. ὅρκον **receive** an oath, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1377a8; λ. πιστά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.5, +others}}; λ. λόγον **demand** an account, τινος for a thing, παρά τινος from a person, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.4.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.47.}}
**II.1.g** λ. ἐν γαστρί **conceive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.24}}; κῦμα λ., of the earth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 128.}}
**II.1.h** **receive as produce, profit**, etc., οἶνον ἐκ τοῦ χωρίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1123; [χρήματα] ἐκ τῆς ἀρχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 347b}}; λ. ἑκατὸν τῆς δραχμῆς, ὀβολοῦ, **purchase** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1263, “Ra.” 1235, compare “Nu.” 1395}}; πόθεν ἄν τις τοῦτο τὸ χρῖμα λάβο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.4.}}
**i** λ. πεῖράν τινος, see at {πεῖρα}.
**2** **admit of**, ὁ μέγας κίνδυνος ἄναλκιν οὐ φῶτα λαμβάνει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.81.}}
**2.b** **admit, initiate**, τοὺς ἐς τὰ τῆς τέχνης εἰλημμένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 17.}}
**3** of persons conceiving feelings and the like, λ. θυμόν **take** heart, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.461: frequently in periphrasis, λ. φόβον, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 729}}]; αἰδῶ λ., ={αἰδεῖσθαι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 345}}; λ. ὀργήν, ={ὀργίζεσθαι}, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1050: so generally λ. ἀρχήν, ={ἄρχεσθαι}, [prev. author] “IA” 1124}}]; λ. ὕψος, ἐπίδοσιν, αὔξησιν, ={ὑψοῦσθαι, ἐπιδιδόναι, αὐξάνεσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.10, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 732b5, etc.; λ. κακόν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1310}}; λ. νόσον **take** a disease, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 610d}}; λ. μορφήν, τέλος, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 762a13, 744a21, etc.}}; αἱ οἰκίαι ἐπάλξεις λαμβάνουσαι **receiving** battlements, **having** battlements **added**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.69, compare 115.}}
**4** with _infinitive_, **receive permission to.. **, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“SIG” 996.6 (Smyrna, 1st c.AD).}}
**B** _middle_, **take hold of, lay hold on**, with _genitive_, [σχεδίης] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.325}}; τῆς κεφαλῆς, τῶν γουνάτων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64, 9.76; χειρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 899, etc.; τοῦ βωμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.126, etc.: with double _genitive_, μου λαβόμενος τῆς χειρός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 153b.}}
**B.2** **seize and keep hold of, obtain possession of**, ἀρχῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 373}}; καιροῦ λαβόμενος **seizing** the opportunity, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.28; λ. ἀληθείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 309d: rarely with _accusative_, τόν.. λελαβέσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.388.}}
**B.3** **lay hands upon**, χαλεπῶς λαμβάνεσθαί τινος **lay** rough **hands on** him, **deal** hardly **with** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. δ.}}
**B.4** of place, λ. τῶν ὀρῶν **take to** the mountains, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24, compare 106}}; Δήλου λαβόμεναι (i.e. αἱ νῆες) **reaching** Delos, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.80.}}
**B.5** **find fault with, censure**, τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 637c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.22.}}
**B.6** λαβέσθαι ἑαυτοῦ **check** oneself, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.24.}}

2985:
** λαμπάς**, άδος, ἡ,
**A** **torch**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 433, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24, etc.; πευκίνη λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1198}}; **beacon-light**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 8, 28, etc.}}; λαμπάδας ἅψασθαι light **torches**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 655}}; λαμπάδας τινάσσων, in Bacchic ceremonies, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 340 (Lyric poetry); used in festal processions, φαίνετε τούτῳ (i.e. τῷ Αἰσχύλῳ) λαμπάδας ἱεράς [prev. work] 1525 (_anapaest meter_),compare “Th.” 102 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.2** **faggot**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.93.4}}; any **light, lamp**, λαμπάδες ἀργυραῖ {{LXX|LXX.Jdt.10.22}}; **wax-light**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.263f; λ. κηροχίτων “Anthologia Graeca” 6.249 (Antip.)}}; later of **oillamps,** {{NT|NT.Matt.25.3.}}
**A.3** _metaphorically_, of the sun, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 10.3, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 879 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; ἡ 'πιοῦσα λ. the coming **light**, i.e. the next day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 352}}; of lightning, δαμασθεὶς λαμπάσιν κεραυνίοις {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 1011, compare “Ba.” 244, 594 (Lyric poetry)}}; of the Cyclops' eye, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 459.}}
**A.4** **meteor**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395b11, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.66, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.25.}}
**A.II** **torch-race**, ={λαμπαδηδρομία}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.105, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.52}}; λαμπάδα δραμεῖν, τρέχειν, run **the race**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1203, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 27.4; τὰς λ. δραμεῖν “IG” 22.1030.9; ἐν ταῖς λ. διηγωνίσθαι [prev. work]1039.20; λαμπάδα φέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1087 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἀφιεμένην τὴν λ. θεῶ see the start,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 131; τὰς λ. συντελεῖν “IG” 22.1011.9; λ. ἔσται.. ἀφ᾽ ἵππων τῇ θεῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328a}}; λαμπάδι νικᾶν win in it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.42, compare “IG” 22.957, +others; λαμπάδα ν. win it, [prev. work]3.106, +others; οἱ νικήσαντες τὴν λ. [prev. work]122,compare “Milet.” 1(7).203a14 (2nd c.BC).}}
**A.II.2** _metaphorically_, of life, λαμπάδα γὰρ ζωᾶς με δραμεῖν.. ἤθελε δαίμων {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 231 (from Chios)}}; καθάπερ λαμπάδα τὸν βίον παραδιδόντας ἄλλοις ἐξ ἄλλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 776b.}}
**A.III** ={λυχνὶς ἀγρία}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.101.}}

2986:
** λαμπρός**, ά, όν,
_feminine_ -ή in _Epic dialect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.269, 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 3.3), but -ά in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 19, 371}}:— **bright, radiant**, of the sun and stars, λ. φάος ἠελίοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.605; ἀστήρ 4.77}}; -ότατος, of Sirius, {{Refs|22.30 (and of the same, λαμπρὸν παμφαίνῃσι 5.6)}}; λαμπρὰ σελήνη Hes.[same places],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44; πρὶν ἡμέραν λ. γενέσθαι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.27}}; of the eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1483, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1045, etc., see below 11.3}}; of metallic bodies, λ. φάλοι, κόρυθες, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.132, 17.269}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, θώρηκες λαμπρὸν γανόωντες {{Refs|13.265.}}
**2** of white cloths and the like, **bright**, λαμπρὸς δ᾽ ἦν ἠέλιος ὥς [ὁ χιτών] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.234; δέρμα.. -ότατον λευκότητι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64}}; λ. ἐσθής, = Latin _toga candida_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.5.1.}}
**3** of water, **clear, limpid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 695, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.19}}; of air, λ. ἠήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 15; αἰθήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 829 (_superlative_, Lyric poetry).}}
**4** of sound or voice, **clear, distinct**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 51d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.199; λαμπρὰ κηρύσσειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 864; φωνὴ -οτέρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 545a12}}; opposed to φ. ἀσαφής, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Aud.” 801b22; λαμπρὸν ἀνολολύξαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.768d; compare λάμπω 1.2.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, of **vigorous** action, λ. ἄνεμος **a keen** wind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1180}}; λ. ἤδη καὶ μέγας καθιείς swooping down like a **fresh** and mighty breeze, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 430, compare 760}}; λαμπρὸς φανήσεται he will come **furiously** forth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 280}}; λ. μάχη a **keenly contested** battle, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.12.5; - ότερος κίνδυνος [prev. author] 1.45.9.}} _adverb_ -ρῶς, ἐπικείμενοι **vigorously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71}}; **utterly**, λ. ἡττῆσθαι, λ. περιεστοιχίσθαι, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.4, 9.1.}}
**6** _metaphorically_ also, **clear, manifest**, μαρτύρια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 797; ταῦτ᾽ ἐπειδὴ λαμπρὰ συμβαίνει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1174; ἴχνη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.5; γεγενημένης τῆς νίκης λ. ἤδη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.55}}; λ. φυγή **decisive**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.11.3}}. _adverb_ - ρῶς, κοὐδὲν αἰνικτηρίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 833; λελυμένων λ. τῶν σπονδῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7; λ. νικᾶν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.10.4}}; λαμπρῶς ἐλέγετο it was said **without concealment**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.67.}}
**II** of persons, **well-known, illustrious** by deeds, station, etc., λ. ἐν τῇσι Ἀθήνῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125; ἐν τοῖσι πολέμοισι ἐὼν -ότατος [prev. author] 7.154}}; λ. ἐν [τοῖς κινδύνοις] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.269; - οτάτους γενομένους τῶν καθ᾽ ἑαυτούς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138; ἐξ ἀδόξων γενέσθαι λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.89; λ. ἐς γένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 37; ἐν λόγοις [prev. author] “Supp.” [902]}}; as honorary title, -ότατος, = Latin _clarissimus,_ {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.911, 7.91, etc.}}; of cities, councils, etc., ἡ λαμπρὰ τῶν Μιλησίων μητρόπολις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 906 A 4 (4th c.AD), compare 867.4 (_superlative_, Ephesus, 2nd c.AD)}}; of actions, etc., ἔργον οὐδὲν ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν λ. γίνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72; τὸν βίον λ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1144; τὸ λ. φῶς ἀποσβεννὺς γένους “Tragica Adespota” 9.}}
**II.2** **magnificent, munificent**, λ. ἐν ταῖς λειτουργίαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.56, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.153 (_superlative_)}}; ὁ λ. καὶ πλούσιος οὗτος {{Refs|[prev. work] 174.}} _adverb_ - ρῶς, χορηγεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1122b22.}}
**II.3** **bright, joyous**, λ. ὥσπερ ὄμματι, of the bearer of good news, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 81, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.10}}; λαμπρὸν ἐξέπεμψα **with bright hopes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1130; λ. ταῖς ἐλπίσιν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.64b}}; also ὄμματι δέρκομαι λαμπρόν, of one **clear in conscience**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.66.}}
**III** of outward appearance, **splendid, brilliant**, νυμφίον.. λ. ὄντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 859}}; of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 22.956.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.1; in dress, [prev. author] “Cyn.” 2.4.5 (_superlative_)}}; of youthful bloom, ὥρᾳ ἡλικίας λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}; of **healthy** look, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 24}} of property, dress, etc., εἴ τί γ᾽ ἔστι λ. καὶ καλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 144, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 316.5; κατασκευή 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.4 (_comparative_)}}; λ. κάλλος **beaming** beauty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250b, etc.: more generally λ. τι ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.15}}; τὸ λ. **splendour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.34; λ. γενέσθαι βουλόμεσθα τοὺς γάμους Euangelus Comicus 1.3}}. _adverb_ -ῶς, opposed to λιτῶς, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 30}}: _superlative_ -ότατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.1}}; later -οτάτως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“JHS” 44.26 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD).}}
**III.2** of language, **brilliant**, τῶν διθυράμβων τὰ λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1388}}; λ. λέξις **ornamental** diction, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1460b4; λόγος 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.9.}}
**IV** Astrology texts, of degrees in a zodiacal sign, ἑκάστου ζῳδίου λαμπρὰς μοίρας ἐξέθεντο {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.1, +others}}
**V** for _adverb_ -ρῶς, see above {{Refs|1.5 and 6, 11.2, 111.1.}}

2987:
** λαμπρότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**brilliancy, splendour**, λ. καὶ τάξις τοῦ στρατεύματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.18}}; of a horse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 11.9}}; of arms, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.9.1, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.14.4.}}
**2** **clearness, distinctness**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 11.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **brilliancy, splendour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.101; ἡ παραυτίκα λ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64, compare 7.69; ἀπὸ οἵας λαμπρότητος.. ἐς οἵαν.. τελευτὴν ἀφῖκτο [prev. author] 7.75, compare 6.31}}: _plural_, **distinctions**, τιμαὶ καὶ λ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.62}}; ἔν τινος λαμπρότητι in **distinction** for a thing, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.16; λ. τῶν πράξεων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.66, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.7.7.}}
**II.2** **munificence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.158.}}
**II.3** **brilliancy** of style, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.25b}}; λαμπρότητες τοῦ λόγου, Latin _lumina orationis_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.23.2.}}
**II.4** λ. ψυχῆς **magnanimity**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.8.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.40.}}
**II.5** as a title, ἡ σὴ λ. your **Serenity, Serene Highness,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|PGrenf. 1.59 (5th-6th c.AD).}}

