**3** of water, **clear, limpid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 695, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.19}}; of air, λ. ἠήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 15; αἰθήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 829 (_superlative_, Lyric poetry).}}
**4** of sound or voice, **clear, distinct**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 51d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.199; λαμπρὰ κηρύσσειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 864; φωνὴ -οτέρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 545a12}}; opposed to φ. ἀσαφής, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Aud.” 801b22; λαμπρὸν ἀνολολύξαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.768d; compare λάμπω 1.2.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, of **vigorous** action, λ. ἄνεμος **a keen** wind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1180}}; λ. ἤδη καὶ μέγας καθιείς swooping down like a **fresh** and mighty breeze, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 430, compare 760}}; λαμπρὸς φανήσεται he will come **furiously** forth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 280}}; λ. μάχη a **keenly contested** battle, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.12.5; - ότερος κίνδυνος [prev. author] 1.45.9.}} _adverb_ -ρῶς, ἐπικείμενοι **vigorously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71}}; **utterly**, λ. ἡττῆσθαι, λ. περιεστοιχίσθαι, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.4, 9.1.}}
**6** _metaphorically_ also, **clear, manifest**, μαρτύρια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 797; ταῦτ᾽ ἐπειδὴ λαμπρὰ συμβαίνει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1174; ἴχνη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.5; γεγενημένης τῆς νίκης λ. ἤδη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.55}}; λ. φυγή **decisive**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.11.3}}. _adverb_ - ρῶς, κοὐδὲν αἰνικτηρίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 833; λελυμένων λ. τῶν σπονδῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7; λ. νικᾶν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.10.4}}; λαμπρῶς ἐλέγετο it was said **without concealment**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.67.}}
**II** of persons, **well-known, illustrious** by deeds, station, etc., λ. ἐν τῇσι Ἀθήνῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125; ἐν τοῖσι πολέμοισι ἐὼν -ότατος [prev. author] 7.154}}; λ. ἐν [τοῖς κινδύνοις] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.269; - οτάτους γενομένους τῶν καθ᾽ ἑαυτούς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138; ἐξ ἀδόξων γενέσθαι λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.89; λ. ἐς γένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 37; ἐν λόγοις [prev. author] “Supp.” [902]}}; as honorary title, -ότατος, = Latin _clarissimus,_ {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.911, 7.91, etc.}}; of cities, councils, etc., ἡ λαμπρὰ τῶν Μιλησίων μητρόπολις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 906 A 4 (4th c.AD), compare 867.4 (_superlative_, Ephesus, 2nd c.AD)}}; of actions, etc., ἔργον οὐδὲν ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν λ. γίνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72; τὸν βίον λ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1144; τὸ λ. φῶς ἀποσβεννὺς γένους “Tragica Adespota” 9.}}
**II.2** **magnificent, munificent**, λ. ἐν ταῖς λειτουργίαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.56, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.153 (_superlative_)}}; ὁ λ. καὶ πλούσιος οὗτος {{Refs|[prev. work] 174.}} _adverb_ - ρῶς, χορηγεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1122b22.}}
**II.3** **bright, joyous**, λ. ὥσπερ ὄμματι, of the bearer of good news, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 81, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.10}}; λαμπρὸν ἐξέπεμψα **with bright hopes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1130; λ. ταῖς ἐλπίσιν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.64b}}; also ὄμματι δέρκομαι λαμπρόν, of one **clear in conscience**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.66.}}
**III** of outward appearance, **splendid, brilliant**, νυμφίον.. λ. ὄντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 859}}; of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 22.956.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.1; in dress, [prev. author] “Cyn.” 2.4.5 (_superlative_)}}; of youthful bloom, ὥρᾳ ἡλικίας λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}; of **healthy** look, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 24}} of property, dress, etc., εἴ τί γ᾽ ἔστι λ. καὶ καλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 144, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 316.5; κατασκευή 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.4 (_comparative_)}}; λ. κάλλος **beaming** beauty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250b, etc.: more generally λ. τι ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.15}}; τὸ λ. **splendour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.34; λ. γενέσθαι βουλόμεσθα τοὺς γάμους Euangelus Comicus 1.3}}. _adverb_ -ῶς, opposed to λιτῶς, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 30}}: _superlative_ -ότατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.1}}; later -οτάτως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“JHS” 44.26 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD).}}
**III.2** of language, **brilliant**, τῶν διθυράμβων τὰ λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1388}}; λ. λέξις **ornamental** diction, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1460b4; λόγος 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.9.}}
**IV** Astrology texts, of degrees in a zodiacal sign, ἑκάστου ζῳδίου λαμπρὰς μοίρας ἐξέθεντο {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.1, +others}}
**V** for _adverb_ -ρῶς, see above {{Refs|1.5 and 6, 11.2, 111.1.}}

2989:
** λάμπω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.474, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_Iterat. λάμπεσκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 84.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus (see. below)}}: _future_ -ψω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 66, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.249 (Antip.)}}: _aorist_ ἔλαμψα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.82 (variant), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 473 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 62, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 335d:}}_perfect_ λέλαμπα (in _present_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1025, “Tr.” 1295 (both Lyric poetry)}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 31.13, etc.:}}_imperfect_ ἐλαμπόμην, _Epic dialect_ λαμπ-, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.319, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1194}}: _future_ λάμψομαι (ἐλλ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80}}:—_passive_, _future_ λαμφθήσομαι (ἐλλ-) {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.3}}: _aorist_ ἐλάμφθην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.10.1}} (περι-): from these late forms of _passive_ must be distinguished the similar _Ionic dialect_ forms of λαμβάνω:—**give light, shine**, of the gleam of arms, τῆλε δὲ χαλκὸς λάμφ᾽ ὥς τε στεροπή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.154, compare 11.66}}; λάμπε δὲ χαλκῷ, of Hector,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|12.463; φῶς λάμπεσκεν 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus [same place]; ἀπ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν δὲ κακὸν πῦρ.. λάμπεσκε 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.19}}; of the eyes, ὀφθαλμὼ δ᾽ ἄρα οἱ πυρὶ λάμπετον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.474}}; of the sun, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.23, etc.}}; of fire, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1007; ἄλσος λάμπεν ὑπὸ δεινοῖο θεοῦ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 71}}:—_middle_, κόρυθος -ομένης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.71; λάμπετο δουρὸς αἰχμή 6.319; δαΐδων ὕπο -ομενάων 18.492, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” (only in this phrase) 19.48, 23.290; χαλκὸς ἐλάμπετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.134}}; of a person, - όμενος πυρί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.623; τεύχεσι λ. 20.46, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 60; ὄσσε -έσθην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.608; πεδίον.. λάμπετο χαλκῷ 20.156, etc.}}
**2** of sound, **ring loud and clear**, παιὰν δὲ λάμπει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 186 (Lyric poetry), compare 473 (Lyric poetry);compare λαμπρός 1.4.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **shine forth, be famous** or **conspicuous**, λάμπει κλέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.23; ἀρετά [prev. author] “I.” 1.22, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 776 (Lyric poetry); δίκα δὲ λάμπει μὲν ἐν δυσκάπνοις δώμασιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 773 (Lyric poetry); τέκνων οἷς ἂν λάμπωσιν νεάνιδες ἧβαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 476 (Lyric poetry); κάλλος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250d.}}
**3.b** Astrology texts, of a planet, **occupy a favourable position**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 51.}}
**4** of persons, φαιδρὸς λάμποντι μετώπῳ with **beaming** face, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 550 (_anapaest meter_)}}; **shine, gain glory**, οὐδ᾽ εἰ Κλέων γ᾽ ἔλαμψε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” [same place]; ἐν ἄλλοις βουσὶν ἰὼν λάμπεσκεν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.141.}}
**II** _transitive_, **cause to shine, illumine**, δόλιον ἀκταῖς ἀστέρα λάμψας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1131 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ion” 83 (_anapaest meter_), “Ph.” 226, “Anthologia Graeca” [where cited], “Tragica Adespota” 33, etc.}}—Found chiefly in poetry and Comedy texts, though the _present_ and _imperfect_ occur in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.11 (_middle_), “Mem.” 4.7.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 419a4}}, and late Prose, and the _aorist_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.82 (variant), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395a15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 3, etc.}}

2990:
** λανθάνω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 75.13, etc.}}:—also **λήθω** (which is the form of the _active_ generally used in compounds, δια-λανθάνω being the sole exception), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.323, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1325 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.48}}; _Doric dialect_ **λάθω** [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 222 (Lyric poetry)}}; _infinitive_
**A ** λᾱθέμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.64}}: _imperfect_ ἐλάνθανον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.721, etc.; ἔληθον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.151, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1359}}; _Epic dialect_ λῆθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.461}}; _Ionic dialect_ λήθεσκεν {{Refs|24.13}}: _future_ λήσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.102, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 98, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _infinitive_ λᾱσην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 22.8}}; _Doric dialect_ λᾱσῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.9, +others, so (in late writers) λήσομαι, see below with 11}}: _aorist 1_ ἔλησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 280 (but 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἐπ-έλησα, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus ἐξ-έλᾱσα, in causal sense)}}: _aorist 2_ ἔλᾰθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.676, etc. (for λέλᾰθον, see infr. B)}}: _perfect_ λέληθα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.9, 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.27}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _participle_ λελᾱθων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.8:}}_pluperfect_ ἐλελήθειν, _Attic dialect_ -ήθη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.33, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 822, “Nu.” 380, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 15}}; _Ionic dialect3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐλελήθεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.79.}}
**B ** causal **ληθάνω**, _aorist 2_ λέλᾰθον, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**C** _middle_ and _passive_, λανθάνομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1455a25 (assuming variant), λήθομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.790, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 39}}; _Doric dialect_ λάθομαι [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.72}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ λανθανόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.227}}: _future_ λήσομαι {{Refs|1.308}}; _Doric dialect_ λᾱσεῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.39, also λελήσομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 198:}}_aorist 1_ ἐλησάμην or λησάμην only in late _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Maiistas Epicus 47, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.62}} (_Doric dialect_ λᾱς-), {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 3.99, etc.}}; also ἐλήσθην, _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ λασθῆμεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.46, compare διαλανθάνω:}}_aorist 2_ ἐλᾰθόμην, _Epic dialect_ λαθ-, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.835, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 289}}: rare in Prose except in compounds, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 38}}; also _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ λελάθοντο, etc., see below c: _perfect_ λέλησμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 342, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 252a}}; _Epic dialect_ λέλασμαι, _participle_ λελασμένος, etc.; compare ἐπιλήθω.
**A** in most of the _active_ tenses, **escape notice** (frequently joined with a _negative_):—Constr.:
**A.1** with _accusative_ _person_ only, **escape** his **notice**, λάθε δ᾽ Ἕκτορα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.277; οὐδέ σε λήσει 23.326; οὐ λῆθε Διὸς πυκινὸν νόον 15.461, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.102, +others; [τοῦτον] οὐκ ἔστι λαθεῖν ὄμματα φωτός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 796 (_anapaest meter_); οὐ λάθει μ᾽ ὀργά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 222 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ph.” 207 (Lyric poetry); τουτί μ᾽ ἐλελήθειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 380}}; εἰ λανθάνει σε perhaps you **don't know**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 78: _impersonal_, λεληθέναι οὐ θαυμάζω τὸ πλῆθος περὶ τούτου}} it **escaped the notice of** the people, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 2.5}}; σὲ δὲ λέληθεν περὶ τοῦτο ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 903c.}}
**A.2** most frequently with a _participle_ added, in which case we usually translate the _participle_ by a Verb, and express λανθάνω by an _adverb_, **unawares, without being observed**; either,
**A.2.a** with _accusative_ _person_, ἄλλον τινὰ λήθω μαρνάμενος I **am unseen by** others while fighting, i.e. {{Refs|1 fight}}**unseen** by them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.273; πάντας ἐλάνθανε δάκρυα λείβων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.93, compare 12.17, 220, 19.88, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.64, 6.36, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.25: frequently in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, μὴ λάθῃ με προσπεσών}} lest he come on **unseen** by me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 46, compare 156 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὅπως μὴ λήσουσιν αὐτοὺς αἱ νῆες.. ἀφορμηθεῖσαι should put to sea **without their observing** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.10}}; or,
**A.2.b** without an _accusative_, φονέα ἐλάνθανε βόσκων he maintained the murderer **unawares**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.44; λέληθας ἐχθρὸς ὤν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 415; δουλεύων λέληθας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 517; συνέβη δὲ ὑπερημέρῳ γενομένῳ λαθεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.89}}: the _reflexive_ _pronoun_ may be supplied and is sometimes added, λέληθεν αὑτὸν τοῖς ξυνοῦσιν ὢν βαρύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 103; ἕως σαυτὸν λάθοις διαρραγείς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 32, compare “Nu.” 242, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.22: sometimes, however, a different object must be supplied from the context, βάλλοντες ἐλάνθανον (not ἑαυτούς, but Τρῶας) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.721; ἐλάνθανε [πάντας] ἔχων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.5}}; μὴ διαφθαρεὶς λάθῃ [τινὰ ὁ βίος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 506; μὴ λάθῃ [ἡμᾶς] φύγδα βάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 256 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.133, etc.}} —In a few examples this _construction_ is reversed, and λαθών is put in the _participle_, as in our idiom, ἀπὸ τείχεος ἆλτο λαθών (for ἔλαθεν ἁλόμενος) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.390; ἣ.. λήθουσά μ᾽ ἐξέπινες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 532.}}
**A.3** rarely with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, μή σε λαθέτω ὑπερτιθέμεν **let it** not **escape** thee to.., i.e. **forget** not to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.23; ἔλαθεν αὐτὸν σύνθημα δοῦναι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 17}}; σφᾶς λέληθε Θεόδωρον εἶναι **it has been unnoticed** that it was.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.41.1.}}
**A.4** followed by a _relative_ clause, οὐδέ με λήθεις, ὅττι θεῶν τίς σ᾽ ἦγε thou **escapest** me not, **it is** not **unknown** to me, that some god led thee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.563; οὐδέ ἑ λήθει, ὅππως.. 23.323}}; ἐδόκεες θεοὺς λήσειν οἷα ἐμηχανῶ thou thought'st to **escape** the gods' **notice** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106; οὔκουν με.. οἷα πράττεις λανθάνει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 465}}; οὐ λανθάνεις με, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.24, compare “Smp.” 3.6, 13}}; ὁ γείτων λ. τινὰ οὐ μόνον ὅτι πράττει, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174b.}}
**A.5** _absolutely_, **escape notice** or **detection**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 455, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37, 69, +others; λάθε βιώσας 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 551; λανθάνει τὸ οὖρον προσπῖπτον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 464.}}
**B** causal, **make one forget** a thing, with _genitive_ _of things_, in compounds ἐκληθάνω, ἐπι-λήθ; the simple Verb only in _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ _aorist_ 2, ὄφρα.. λελάθῃ ὀδυνάων that.. he **may cause** him **to forget** his pains, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.60; πόλιν λελάθοιτε συντυχιᾶν “Lyrica Adespota” 140.9: but}}
**B.II** in late _Epic dialect_, λέλαθον, ={ἔλαθον}, **escaped notice of**, ἑὸν νόον, τοκῆας, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.226, 3.779, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 876.}}
**C** _middle_ and _passive_, **let** a thing **escape one, forget**,
**C.1** **forget** simply, in _present_ (_absolutely_), σὺ δὲ λήθεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.790: with _genitive_, Κίρκης μὲν ἐφημοσύνης.. λανθανόμην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.227, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.72; οὔ ποτε λήσομαι αὐτῶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.308; ἄλγος, οὗ ποτ᾽ οὐ λελήσεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 198}}: mostly in _aorist_ 2, ἀλκῆς λαθέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 731; νόστου τε λαθέσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.97; πῶς ἂν.. Ὀδυσῆος.. λαθοίμη; 1.65}}: also in _reduplicate_ _aorist_, οὐδὲ σέθεν.. θεοὶ μάκαρες λελάθοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.127; μή τίς μοι ἀπειλάων λελαθέσθω 16.200; οὐ δυνάμην λελαθέσθ᾽ Ἄτης 19.136 (but in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 471 like the _active_, ὅπως λελάθοιτο τεκοῦσα}} that she might bear **unknown**): so in _perfect_, τῶν δὲ λέλασται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.834; ἐμεῖο λελασμένος 23.69; κείνου λελῆσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 342, etc.; ἑταίρων πάντων λέλησται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 252a}}: with a _relative_ clause, λελασμένος ὅσσ᾽ ἐπεπόνθει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.92}}: _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, οὐδέ ποτε λησόμενον οἷον ἔφυ κακόν}} never **will be forgotten, El.**1249 (Lyric poetry); compare ἐπιλανθάνω.
**C.2** **forget purposely, pass over**, ἢ λάθετ᾽ ἢ οὐκ ἐνόησεν either he **chose to forget it**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.537; μαθοῦσιν αὐδῶ, κοὐ μαθοῦσι λήθομαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 39.}}
**C.II** in later writers _future_ _middle_ is used like _active_, **escape notice**, ἡμᾶς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 66a31, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.737, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sacr.” 14: _absolutely_, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.52.}}

2992:
** λᾱός**, ὁ,
_Ionic dialect_ **ληός** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 88, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.42}} (variant{λαόν}, which is in all Mss. in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4.148), conjecture in 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 14.9}}; _Attic dialect_ **λεώς**, which is also used in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22, 8.136, while the form λαός is sometimes used in Trag., and once or twice even in Comedy texts (see. below 1.3): also in Inscrr. and Papyrus (see. below)}} and in late Prose, as Foed.Byzantine cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.52.7 (_plural_), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.4.3 (_plural_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1096b, etc. (both forms in proper nouns, Λεωβώτης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.204, Λαβώτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.18, etc.).}}
**1** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, λαός}} (λαοί) usually means **men**, i.e. soldiers, both of the whole army and smaller divisions, κριτὸς ἔγρετο λ. Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs|7.434; λαὸν ἀγείρειν 16.129; πολὺν ὤλεσα λαόν 2.115: _plural_, ἅμα τῷ γε.. ἄριστοι λ. ἕποντ᾽ [prev. work] 578; στίχες ἀσπιστάων λ. 4.91}}; _periphrastic_, στρατὸς λαῶν{{Refs|[prev. work] 76; λαῶν ἔθνος 13.495}}; mostly including both **foot and horse**, as {{Refs|2.809}}; but sometimes λαός denotes **foot**, as opposed to horse, {{Refs|7.342}}; also, **a land army**, opposed to a fleet, {{Refs|4.76, 9.424, 10.14}}; also, **the common men**, opposed to their leaders, {{Refs|2.365, 13.108}}; but
**2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, λαοί, more rarely λαός}}, almost always means **men** or **people**; as **subjects** of a prince, e.g. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.214, 305, +others (λαοί is sometimes so used in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, e.g. 17.226, 24.611}}; λαοὶ ἀγροιῶται country-**folk**, {{Refs|11.676}}; **work-people**, {{Refs|17.390)}}; of **sailors**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.248; so after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ναυτικὸς λεώς}} seafaring **folk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 383; πᾶς ὁ χειρῶναξ λεώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 844; ὁ γεωργικὸς λεώς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 920 (Lyric poetry)}}: in _singular_, **slave**, τὸν Εὐρυσθέως λεών, of Heracles, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 23 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; and so perhaps λεὼς αὔτοικος {{Refs|“GDI” 5533e (from Zeleia): more generally, μέροπες λαοί}}, i.e. **man**kind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 90 (Lyric poetry)}}; λ. ἐγχώριοι the natives,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 517, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.194}}; especially in Egypt, of the fellahin, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 42.11 - 16 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 4.380.5 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; **civil population**, opposed to priests and soldiers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.12 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), compare 225.8 (Milet., 3rd c.BC), +others}}
**3** **people assembled**, as in the theatre, ὁ πολὺς λαῶν ὄχλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 676, compare 219 (both Lyric poetry)}}; especially in the Ecclesia, αἱ στίχες τῶν λαῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 163: hence the phrase ἀκούετε λεῴ}} hear **O people**!—the usual way of beginning proclamations at Athens, like our {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Oyez” ! 6th c.BC: Susario Comicus 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 551, “Av.” 448}}; τιμῶσιν οἱ πάντες λεῴ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1275; δεῦρ᾽ ἴτε, πάντες λεῴ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 384; Ἀττικὸς λεώς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 681}}; ὁ πολὺς λεώς **the multitude**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 458d, etc.}}
**4** in LXX, of **the** **people**, as opposed to priests and Levites, {{NT|“1 Es.” 5.46; in “NT”, of “Jews”, opposed to Gentiles, NT.Matt.2.6, NT.Luke.2.10, +others, compare “SIG” 1247 (Jewish tombstones)}}; of {{NT|“Christians”, opposed to heathen, NT.Act.15.14, +others}}
**II** **a people**, i.e. all who are called by one name, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, Δωριεῖ λαῷ “O.” 8.30; Λυδῶν δὲ λαὸς καὶ Φρυγῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 770; ξύμπας Ἀχαιῶν λαός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1243, compare “OT” 144, etc.}}; ἱππόται λαοί, i.e. the Thessalians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.153, compare 9.54, “N.” 1.17. (The resemblance between λαός}} **people** and λᾶος **stone** (compare λᾶας) is implied in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.611 λαοὺς δὲ λίθους ποίησε Κρονίων (in the story of Niobe)}}; and so {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} explains the word from the legend of Deucalion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“O.” 9.46, compare 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 122, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.7.2; but compare 4th c.BC: Philochorus Historicus 12.)}} (From λᾱϝ-, as shown by the _proper noun _ Λαϝοπτόλεμος {{Refs|“GDI” 3151, ϝιόλαϝος [prev. work]3132 (from Corinth): hence probably λήϊτον.)}}

2995:
** λάρυγξ** [ᾰ], υγγος, ὁ,
**larynx** or **upper part of the windpipe**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493a6}}; used in sounding the vowels,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 535a32: but in Poets confused with φάρυγξ (gullet) compare (4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 535a29), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 158; χωρεῖν κατὰ τοῦ λ. 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.7, compare 4th c.BC: Crobylus Comicus 8}}; of gluttons, ἀνόσιοι λάρυγγες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 139; ἐκ τοῦ λ. ἐκκρεμάσας τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1363; τὸν λάρυγγ᾽ ἂν ἐκτέμοιμί σου [prev. author] “Ra.” 575: _metaphorically_, λ. γλυκύς}} **speech**, {{LXX|LXX.Sir.6.4.}}
**II** ={τραχεῖα ἀρτηρία}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Meno Medicus “Iatrika” 8.30.}}

2997:
** λάσκω**,
_imperfect_ ἔλασκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1214 (Lyric poetry)}}: _future_ λᾰκήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 381, 384:}}_aorist 1_ ἐλάκησα [ᾰ{{Refs|] [prev. work] 382 (δια-λᾱκήσας [prev. author] “Nu.” 410 is probably from διαλᾱκέω, _Doric dialect_ for -ληκέω):}}_aorist 2_ ἔλᾰκον, _Epic dialect_ λάκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.25, +others}}: _aorist 1_ ἔληξα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 8.65}}: _perfect_ λέληκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.141, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 618b31, λέλᾱκα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 407 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 410 (_paratragoedia_)}}; _participle_ _feminine_ λελᾰκυῖα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.85:}} —_middle_, see below {{Refs|11}}:—**ring, rattle, crash**:
**I** of things, **ring** when struck, λάκε χαλκὸς νυσσομένων ξίφεσίν τε καὶ ἔγχεσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.25; λάκε δ᾽ ἀσπίς 20.277}}; also λάκε δ᾽ ὀστέα the bones **cracked, broke with a crash**, {{Refs|13.616}}; λάκε δ᾽ ἀμφὶ πυρὶ ὕλη **crackled**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 694}}; ἔλακον ἀξόνων βριθομένων χνόαι **creaked** under the weight, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 153 (Lyric poetry):}} —this sense occurs only in _aorist 2_ _active_
**II** of animals, **scream**, of the falcon, ὀξὺ λεληκώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.141}}; of the nightingale in the falcon's talons, τί λέληκα; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 207}}; also, of dogs, **howl, bay**, Σκύλλη.. δεινὸν λελακυῖα Od.[same place]; rare in Prose, οὐ μινυρίζει οὐδὲ λέληκεν, of the black eagle, Arist.[same place]:—this sense occurs only in _perfect_, except in _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _middle_, κύνες λελάκοντο {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 145.}}
**III** of human beings, **shout, scream, cry aloud**, λέληκεν ἢν καὶ μηδέν᾽ ἀνθρώπων ὁρᾷ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.15; φόβος μυχόθεν ἔλακε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 35 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1094, etc.}}; τί λέλακα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ach.” [same place]; μή νυν λακήσῃς [prev. author] “Pax” 382}}: hence of Oracles, **utter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1426 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 824 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 39}}; also, **sing**, πρὸς αὐλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 346.}}
**III.2** with _accusative_ _cognate_, **shriek forth, utter aloud**, ὀλολυγμόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 596}}; στονόεν λέλακε χώρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” [same place]; λ. βοάν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “El.” [same place], compare “Ion” 776 (_anapaest meter_); ἀγγελίας [prev. author] “IT” 461 (_anapaest meter_); πῆμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 865; ῥῆμα γενναῖον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 97: with double _accusative_, τοιαῦτα λάσκεις τοὺς.. φίλου; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 671:—in this sense only in Trag. and (rarely) Comedy texts}}

2998:
** λᾱτομ-έω**,
**quarry**, γῆν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 57 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; πέτραν “IG” 42(1).122.25 (Epid.), compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.39; λίθους “PCair.Zen.” 499.38 (3rd c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 161: _absolutely_, “PCair.Zen.” 296.34 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 25, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.2.9}}:—_passive_, λελατόμηται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ -ούμενα θραύματα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.12.}}
**II** λ. λάκκον **hew** it **out**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.21.33, cf. LXX.Deu.6.11 (_passive_).}}

2999:
** λατρ-εία**, ἡ,
**the state of a hired labourer, service**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 966; ἐπίπονον ἔχειν λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 830 (Lyric poetry): _plural_, οἵας λατρείας ἀνθ᾽ ὅσου ζήλου τρέφει [prev. author] “Aj.” 503, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 225 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: _metaphorically_, **the business** or **duties** of life, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.107c.}}
**2** λ. τοῦ θεοῦ, θεῶν, **service to** the gods, divine **worship**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23c, “Phdr.” 244e (_plural_): _absolutely_, LXX.Exo.12.25, +others, NT.Rom.9.4, etc.}}
